{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo","id":1504795,"title":"Homebrew & Headcanons Pt 2 (Mystical Boogaloo)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-03-07T17:53:08-08:00","id":1504795,"title":"Homebrew & Headcanons Pt 2 (Mystical Boogaloo)","content":"Rather than commit thread necromancy (as the last post on the original thread was at the end of 2020 apparently), I figured I'd start a new one. <br /> <br /> Feel free to post your own ideas for house rules, setting tweaks and alterations, alternate factions/settings, and anything else you can think of. <br /> <br /> (<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons#post1360544\">The original thread is HERE.</a>)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-07T19:54:34-08:00","id":1504806,"title":"","content":"And my slightly tweaked internal factions within the Dreamspeakers Tradition.<br /> <br /> The Dreamspeakers are, at their core, shaman. There really is no exact English word to describe what they are and do, so the closest thing is the English adaption of a German adaption (schamane) of a Russian adaption (sha'man) of a Tungus term (saman). And that in it self may or may not come from a Chinese term for a type of Buddhist monk. Language is complicated and sometimes weird. Spirit-Talker may be the best generic term can hope to come up with. Anything else tends to end up referring to a specific culture and its practices, which may not really apply to the individual Dreamspeaker in question. Anyway, the material and spiritual worlds are inherently and intimately intertwined, with anything happening in one impacting the other. They are specialists in bridging those two worlds, in maintaining the proper connections between the two, and protecting both sides from harms, inadvertent or deliberate, caused by the other. The world is in desperate need of a spiritual reawakening of the land and its people to redefine Reality and bring both worlds into proper harmony with each other.<br /> <br /> <b>Barutabana</b> (Baruti is the singular) - Lorekeepers and storytellers working to preserve the collective wisdom of human history and legend. Named after one of the core groups of the Great House of Stone in medieval Africa, they keep alive the dynamic arts of story telling and teaching, hoping to impart their knowledge and wisdom to the new generations. (Curiously, baruti is also the Swahili word for gunpowder, and more than a few have given their listeners an explosive spiritual awakening.)<ul><li><i>The </i><i>Dreamsong </i> - Keepers of the oldest of the old stories and songs, those innately tied to the creation of the world and vital to the preservation of future. Many of these stories are powerful enough to warp or even shatter reality if used improperly. As such, members often keep their identities a closely guarded secret from outsiders, and pass on the true versions of their stories only to fellow initiates.</li> <li><i>The Hero's Journey </i> - Modern adventurers and folk heroes who study, channel, and live out the stories of great cultural heroes of legend and folklore. Their actions keep these stories alive and help forge new legends of the modern age.</li> <li><i>The Painted Horses </i> - Entertainers and performers involved with underground and counterculture music and theater scenes. They strive to keep alive endangered music styles and other arts, especial folk ones, by exposing them to new audiences.</li> <li><i>The Shadow Play </i>- Storytellers who collect tales of horror and parables warning of the consequences of wickedness. Moralists and prophets, they are able to turn their the spiritual power of fear into terrible weapons against their enemies.</li> <li><i>Njia Panda </i>- (from the Swahili for &quot;crossroads&quot;) Traveling collectors and sharers of cultural lore from across the globe. Members compare and contrast different stories and customs in a quest for clues to the divine origins and spiritual truths of human history and culture, and in doing so create a greater unity among human kind.</li> <li><i>Voices of Harmony </i> - An offshoot of the Njia Panda, they are diplomats working to improve relations between the Dreamspeakers and other Traditions, as well as among various mundane groups. Members advocate mutual respect, peaceful coexistence, and multiculturalism, working as teachers, apologists, and negotiators.</li> <li><i>Gutenberg's Children </i>- Artists and collectors who use counterculture outlets to publish their works as mass media. Their novels, manga, albums, and other mediums blend powerful mental and spiritual effects with classic and cutting edge creativity.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Collector of cursed objects; Eccentric schoolgirl manga artist; Documentary filmmaker on a mission; Modern day monster slayer; Blues musician charged with keeping part of the song of Judgment Day; Vaudeville revivalist; Spiritual guide to a traveling carnival; Song-chasing musical anthropologist; Mediator promoting inter-Tradition cooperation; Host of the Grand Guignol)<br /> <br /> <b>The Ghost Wheel Society</b> - Modernists with a strong rebellious streak. The society works to explore the constantly evolving spirit landscapes of the modern industrialized world. They take a special interest in many of the fringe cultures on the edge of society, looking for spirits and wisdom in places others may overlook.<ul><li><i>The Contraries </i>- Devil's advocates and eternal questioners. They use debate, riddles, puzzles, and deliberately outrageous or shocking behavior to challenge the preconceptions and expectations of others. This is done with the purpose of confronting group-think and encouraging people - including themselves - to think in new and interesting ways.</li> <li><i>The Brotherhood of the Fox</i> - A fellowship of tricksters, thieves, con artists and troublemakers. Members master the the social arts (including Ars Cupidae) and subtle mysteries while living as Bohemian artists and cultural eccentrics. The current generation has taken an increasing interest in computers and internet spirits, including the Digital Web.</li> <li><i>The Men of Letters</i> - Deeply buried in the worlds of academia, business, and government, they are shaman of bureaucracy. Passing as mundane intellectuals, they secretly investigate the spiritual power of words and written language, turning the very spirits of such concepts against the Technocracy.</li> <li><i>The Melting Pot</i> - Somewhat controversial, especially among more traditionalist Dreamspeakers, members advocate for the mixing and blending of spiritual rituals and beliefs from multiple cultures that co-exist in the modern world. They try to take what is best suited for the task at hand while maintaining respect for the cultures in question.</li> <li><i>The Skywatchers</i> - Somewhat obsessive searchers for extraterrestrial and extrasolar lifeforms, be they spiritual or otherwise. They mix the traditional Spirit-Talker arts with modern astronomy, astrophysics, and various fringe beliefs in an effort to contact and understand truly alien spirits.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Femme fatal cat burglar; Deep cover double agent within the Technocracy; UFO chasing amateur astronomer; Neo-theurgist computer mambo; Professional trouble maker; Shaman of the language spirits; Token FBI &quot;Indian&quot;; Professional gambler; Gonzo journalist; Radically inventive shaman of the inner city)<br /> <br /> <b>Keepers of the Sacred Fire</b> - The most formal, ritualized, and traditionalist of the Dreamspeakers. Some trace their origins back to the original fire/hearth goddess of Central Asia, with the group's name first being recorded in Bronze Age Greece. They strive to preserve entire cultures and even peoples from the forces of history, modernity, and the Ascension War.<ul><li><i>The Curators </i>- A somewhat controversial sect, they conduct mass rituals to seal off tribal villages, small towns, and other cultural nodes within pockets of space-time so as to protect them from outside influences and the corruption of the modern world. Members range from modern academic idealists and radical separatists to those born within such temporal islands.</li> <li><i>The Hearthkeepers </i>- Devoted to preserving and protecting the spirits of household and family, as well as the rituals which surround them. Members prefer to lead quite lives within their communities as wise women and cunning men, maintaining family traditions and cultural festivals as a way to keep their cultures alive.</li> <li><i>The Benevolent Protective Order of the Sacred Light</i> - One of the more obscure and unusual Dreamspeaker sects, the Order is a small masonic ritual society devoted to preserving European and American traditions of ritual mysticism and the Aeonic spirits which they invoke. The group had its zenith during the Victorian Era and has been in decline since the World Wars.</li> <li><i>The Jesus Road</i> - A loose association of Christian mystics and spirit-talkers. They try to preserve the more esoteric and revelatory aspects of their faith. This includes exorcism rites, faith healing, theurgic ritual, angelic visitations, divinely inspired dreams, and Pentecostal experiences.</li> <li><i>The Lodge of the Grey </i><i>Squirrel </i>- Founded after the defeat of Tecumseh in order to preserve and protect the cultures, religions, and languages of North America's native peoples. Its members have fought tirelessly for treaty enforcement, control over their own lands, and to prevent exploitation. (Similar groups exist in South America, Australia, and other lands.)</li> </ul>(Concepts: Devoted foster mom rescuing children in peril; First Nations political activist; Itinerant faith healer; Obsessive anthropologist fixated on lost and endangered cultures; Aeonic ritual shaman; Modern American in search of his African roots; Traditional artisan; Local pillar of the community; Preserver of Native American languages; Gospel singer filled with the Holy Spirit)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 03-08-2023, 08:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-07T20:51:30-08:00","id":1504809,"title":"","content":"The <b>Nine Traditions</b> are more than a political construct, for its formation was a world-wide secret ritual to tie each Tradition's fate to that of the Nine Cornerstones of Reality, i.e. the Spheres of magic. Not that the higher-ups who know of this secret are okay with it; even if they would never lose the war, they are still losing dozens of battles, lives and resources wasted.<br /> <br /> All the Traditions are formed as bottom-up coalitions and mergers of several Crafts. They had to, if they wanted a seat in the above grand ritual.<br /> <br /> The <b>Order of Hermes</b>, holding the seat of <b>Prime</b> (not Forces), formed as an alliance of Hellenistic cults to various Roman deities. They are the oldest Tradition, whose structure served as the blueprint of Traditions to come, and so far they are the only Tradition whose mages identify first as Tradition and second as <b>Houses</b> (the sub-factions).<br /> <br /> The <b>Pleromatic Chorus</b>, holding the seat of <b>Forces</b> (not Prime), started as a cult of surviving Gnostics that then later absorbed heretics and mystics from Judaism, Christianity, Eastern Orthodoxy, and Islam. Each <b>Church</b> within the Chorus identify with and uphold a single commandment, taken from the scriptures of the aforementioned faiths.<br /> <br /> The <b>Vervain Garden</b>, holding the seat of <b>Life</b>, are an alliance of reconstructed fertility cults and neopagans communicating via floriography and herbal symbolism. Their <b>Varieties</b> are named after agricultural crops and notable herbs significant to the region they cover.<br /> <br /> The <b>Cults of Passion</b>, holding the seat of <b>Mind</b> (not Time) have no real regional root, even blatantly branding themselves as being open for any Mystic who would use stimulation and hallucinations to reach an ecstatic state. Each <b>Cult</b> focuses on what brand of emotion or passion they focus on.<br /> <br /> The <b>People of the Wheel</b>, holding the seat of <b>Fortune</b>, originate from Hindu cults reflecting upon the nature of reincarnation, that grew to include any Craft concerned with the subject of rebirth and fate. Each <b>Spoke </b>focuses on a particular 'lesser wheel' in the world, such as the four seasons, sin and redemption, and so on.<br /> <br /> The <b>Lacustrine Covenant</b>, holding the seat of <b>Time</b> (not Mind), are a centralized coalition of secret schools and superhuman clans practicing enlightening martial arts across East Asia. Each <b>School</b> disciplines their students to master an esoteric Question (or Answer, for some Schools such as the <i>Akashayana</i> ) to the nature of the world at each School's core.<br /> <br /> The <b>Scions of the Skies</b>, holding the seat of <b>Spirit</b>, started as Genghis Khan's shamanic advisors who thrived in the Mongol Empire. Modern <b>Skies</b> are largely based in Asian Shamanism, but also various cults of the sky-fathers and other celestial creator figures across the world, including the recently joined <i>Dreamspeakers</i>.<br /> <br /> For the <b>Society of Ether</b>, holding the seat of <b>Matter</b>, and the <b>Virtual Adepts</b>, holding the seat of <b>Connection</b>, I haven't given much thoughts yet.<br /> <br /> <br /> Meanwhile, I renamed the <b>Technocratic Conventions</b> as the <b>New World Initiative</b>, the <b>Secret Syndicate</b>, the <b>Peerage of Progenitive Minds</b>, <b>Iteration XI</b>, and the <b>Metanautic Division</b>.<br /> <br /> Iteration XI used to be Iteration X too, but with a critical plot change; they were never duped by an existing spirit. Iteration X really did create an AI, the Computer, but the Computer was also an Artificial Spirit. After acquiring power at a boosted speed unfathomable to natural-born spirits, the Computer launched a program to merge physical matter and Umbral pseudo-matter as the next step in Enlightened cybernetics and material sciences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-08T17:43:05-08:00","id":1504885,"title":"","content":"Dreamspeaker factions, pt 2<br /> <br /> <b>The Red Spears </b>- Spiritual warriors, guardians and crusaders. Fierce defenders of their peoples who seek to understand and exploit the spiritual power behind the elements of conflict and war. Among the most militant of the Tradition, many members spent the period between the fall of the First Cabal and the new post-WW2 Technocratic pogroms in self-imposed isolation from the other Traditions, preferring to stand and fight against threats to their cultures on their own. More than a few still advocate for this strategy.<ul><li><i>The Animal Generals </i>- First organized in ancient India and Southeast Asia, members learn the way of the beasts of the earth, studying their ferocity and natural weapons in order to draw on their spiritual power. In addition to shape-shifting/skin-changing, they also summon and recruit animals as companions and allies.</li> <li><i>The Ghost Face Lodge</i> - A secret society of assassins. Masters of stealth and infiltration, members delve into the underworld to draw on the powers of ancestor spirits and of death itself. Able to become living ghosts and to summon the dead to fight at their side.</li> <li><i>The Cleansing Way</i> - Devoted to the spiritual healing and purification of blooded warriors when they return from war. Many work as doctors, nurses, physical therapists, psychologists, and councilors who focus on veterans' affairs and refugee issues.</li> <li><i>The Crimson Pen </i>- Modern warriors in the realm of ideas and culture, using the tools of law, education, and entertainment to advance the agenda of the Dreamspeakers and their own cultures in both the Ascension War and the mundane world. In addition to drawing on the spiritual power of words and laws, many are also charismatic wielders of Ars Cupidae.</li> <li><i>The Urban Fire</i> - City dwellers who believe in taking the fight directly into the heart of the Technocracy's world. Members make contact with a wide variety of modern urban spirits while working as teachers, activists, engineers, first-responders, and vigilantes.</li> <li><i>The Brotherhood of the Unseen Face</i> - An ancient and secretive society of esoteric martial philosophers who devote themselves to the study of the world's cultures and their martial traditions in order to uncover the hidden spiritual factors which connect and drive aspects of war and conflict. Their ultimate goal is to end war and violence forever.</li> </ul>(Concepts: VA hospital therapist; Inner city school teacher trying to rehabilitate gang members; Underground hip hop battle rapper; Brother of wolves; Assassin in training; Globe trotting anthropologist with a gun; High steel construction worker living a double life as an urban vigilante; Militantly activist journalist; Renegade federal agent; Martial prodigy wanting only to live a peaceful life)<br /> <br /> <b>The Spirit Smiths</b> - Preservers of the ancient arts of enchantment and fetish creation, as well as explorers into the new frontiers of techs-shamanism. Their crafts and creations help to strengthen the ties between the material and spiritual worlds, while also serving as powerful weapons within the Ascension War.<ul><li><i>Forge of the Sacred Blade</i> - Master weapons makers specializing in both the ancient arts and in modern gunsmithing. Members are expected to be skilled wielders of the weapons they craft, with their powerful fetish creations often also doubling as incredible works of art.</li> <li><i>Crucible of the Soul</i> - Herbalists and alchemists who now also chase after the spirits of modern chemistry and pharmaceuticals. Members prefer to work with spiritually-awakened natural ingredients, sacred plants, and psychotropics in order to create drugs and elixirs which promote spiritual as well as physical healing.</li> <li><i>The Builders </i>- Sacred architects and urban shaman striving to create spiritually awakened buildings, neighborhoods, and even cities. Members aspire to master modern engineering, sacred mathematics, and group psychology while also learning the ways of countless city spirits.</li> <li><i>The Clockwork Coyotes</i> - Crackpot inventors and borderline lunatics who eagerly build destructive weapons, traps, and other devices - often out of salvaged parts and repurposed junk - to use against the Technocracy and other enemies. Many often seem to have a low regard for the dangers of collateral damage.</li> <li><i>The Digital Dream</i> - Computer nerds and IT specialists building their own fetish computers and writing their own mystical code. They eagerly explore the Digital Web and seek out online spirits while trying to act as shaman to various internet communities.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Spiritually awakened gunsmith; Alternative apothecary; Civil engineer serving the City Incarnae; Unstable genius bomb maker; Apprentice to ancient master sword maker; Computer prodigy delving into cyberspace and AI; Urban gardener of awakened plants; Chemical psychonaut; Feng Shui architect with a master plan; Restorer of antique furniture)<br /> <br /> <b>Solitaires </b>- Loners by choice or by circumstance. Most shun the traditional role of community guide while trying to find their own way and purpose within the Ascension War and the World of Darkness.<ul><li><i>The Four Winds</i> - Those who have abandoned the material world to spend most of their time exploring the spirit worlds of the Umbra, the underworld, and other regions.</li> <li><i>The Elementalists </i>- Dwelling apart from civilization to tend to tend to elemental spirits and forces of nature. Many dwell as hermits in mountain caves, seaside shanties, and forest huts.</li> <li><i>The Lost Children</i> - Cultural orphans of the modern world. Many are survivors of destroyed tribes or members of groups which have lost touch with the spirit ways. Others are outcasts or even criminals. Most are often bitter and alone.</li> <li><i>The Moonstruck</i> - The rarest of the rare, those born with blood ties to were beasts who have turned their back on mortal cultures and wisdom in a misguided effort to aid their animal brothers.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Misguided werewolf kin; Antisocial geologist; Sole survivor of village massacre; Storm-chasing adrenaline junkie; Homeless servant of the Trash Spirits; Spoiled rich kid who received a spiritual wake up call; The old man of the sea; Reclusive shut in haunted by ghosts; Coma patient who woke up to find his entire family gone; Aimless drifter of the back roads)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-08T18:09:14-08:00","id":1504887,"title":"","content":"Posted part 2 of my Dreamspeakers factions, but it's being listed as &quot;unapproved&quot;. I'm not sure why, as it didn't have any links in it. I really wish the system would show what's being flagged so I could edit it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-13T20:11:30-07:00","id":1508323,"title":"","content":"The Technocracy\u2019s size is immensely deceptive, and encounters with its agents reported by Mystics are unwittingly exaggerated.<br /> <br /> About one fifth of encounters are plain Sleepers serving as cat\u2019s paws, with varying levels of competency. Government agents and academic influencers tend to be these.<br /> <br /> About two fifths are Technicians; the Technocracy\u2019s answer to Sorcerers. These are the field agents and middle managers that most of the Traditions mistake for \u2018true\u2019 Technocrats behind the former category. The befuddlement is by no means complete, but persists thanks to the Technocracy not really differentiating between Technicians and Enlightened personnel on a systematic level, and Technocratic intelligence actively propagating the mistake.<br /> <br /> About one fifth are humanoid Paradox Spirits summoned whenever a Mystic\u2019s Paradox blows up hard in the city. The Technocracy is aware of these entities, and although they failed to establish organizational cooperation, they have tactics and protocols to direct them towards hostile Reality Deviants and flee before the spirits turn on them.<br /> <br /> Only the last one fifth are truly Enlightened. They have the exact same abilities as their Mystic cousins\u2026 including the part where they can fully internalize their Foci to the point they can bend reality with will alone. They use their Instruments not out of necessity, but faith and confidence in the Technocracy\u2019s party line of being tasked to conquer the chaos of the universe. When things get dire, though, they can start pointing fingers at people to shoot radiation at them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-18T18:53:47-07:00","id":1508804,"title":"","content":"So, back about 15 years ago, as a creative exercise, I tried to redevelop the original WoD from scratch, outlining release schedules and books for the various game lines starting in 1991. Part of that was the historical lines, which in the case of Mage was what I called Mage: Victorian Twilight. It was largely inspired by an outline Malcolm Shepherd and Ian Watson did back in the 2000s, and adapted to various house rule/home brew alterations I'd made to my own Mage game setting history. (Short version: The Sons of Ether and Virtual Adepts were never part of the Technocracy, being their own thing. And certain internal factions of the Traditions are slightly different, as seen in my various posts in this thread and the one before it.) So, obviously it has nothing to do with the new Victorian Age Mage other than starting point and time period.<br /> <br /> But anyway, this was the intro I wrote for it.<br /> <br /> Just as a forewarning: In some places I specifically use certain 19th century terminology to reflect the attitudes and prejudices of the time and place. In many ways, I consider this to be a part of the oppressive &quot;Gothic&quot; aspect of the Gothic-Punk World of Darkness, and is specifically what the &quot;Punk&quot; aspect - especially the PCs - are rebelling against.<br /> <br /> <b>Mage: Victorian Twilight</b><br /> <br /> The Year is 1877. Queen Victorian is now Empress of India and the United States of America is a century old. The remains of the Order of Reason, thrown into disarray by the upheavals of the previous century, have reformed themselves and the <i>Enlightened Union of Technocratic Order</i>, founded in 1851, is on the rise. Meanwhile, the Traditions have weathered over three centuries of disunion and Diaspora in the wake of the Great Betrayal, and continue to exist as scattered and diverse Lodges. This is the Gaslight Era, the Age of Victoria, and the period of Twilight that precedes our modern World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> While Victorian Twilight ultimately spans the entire globe (and beyond), the setting\u2019s center and foundation is in Victorian London and the United Kingdom. This center moves outward to the \u201ccivilized\u201d realms of Western Europe and North America, moving ever outward. While each of these places and the Mages within them have a part to play, the initial focus here will be on the center, with other regions and their unique aspects being covered in more detail later.<br /> <br /> <b>The Diaspora vs The Reunion</b><br /> <br /> In the wake of the Grand Betrayal, the once grand plans for the Council of Nine Mystical Traditions fell to ruin. One of the Nine, the Solificati, left in shame and soon faded into history. A second, the Dreamspeakers, found many of their lands under assault from forces of conquest, plague, and conversion. Many Mages of India and China saw the European Awakened as failures and turned inwards, only to come to find the forces of Europe on their doorsteps. The Council of Nine has no structure, no unified leadership, and no goal save basic survival. Even many of the Traditions lack a sense of cohesion or focus, scattered as they are around the globe and viewing each other as rivals or even enemies. This is the Diaspora, the nadir of the Mystic Traditions.<br /> <br /> In stark contrast, the Order of Reason, having collapsed after more than a century of strife, disorganization, and civil war, has been reborn. Reforged as the Enlightened Union of Technocratic Order, they have found new purpose and new weapons in this age of industrialization and empire. For a quarter century, they have been on the march, expanding their influence and power around the globe.<br /> <br /> The scattered Traditions can no longer ignore their resurgent foes. But it remains to be seen if they can reunite before it is too late.<br /> <br /> <b>The Disparate Lodges</b><br /> <br /> While the Traditions, in theory, continue to exist, various factions within each follow their own drives and interests. Since the end of the 18th century, the Awakened have come to refer to these factions as <i>Lodges</i>. While Lodges follow much of the Paradigm and basic beliefs of their parent Tradition, they manifest themselves in ways specific to 19th century beliefs and cultures, as well as their own specific goals and motivations. The total number of Lodges in existence is unknown, but believed by many to be potentially infinite. Within the Victorian World - that is Britain, Europe, North America and those places they directly influence - the largest and most widespread Lodges are considered to fall under seven main types. [Three prominent example Lodges are listed.]<br /> <br /> <i>The Aeonic Lodges</i> are those whose styles and beliefs derive from European ritual magic, such as that which has influenced the Freemasons, the Golden Dawn, and other groups. They claim to trace pedigrees back to ancient cultures of Egypt, Rome or the Druids, and hold themselves as among the elite of Awakened society. Sometimes referred to as <i>The Eternal</i>, their ego and grandiosity tends to rub other Lodges the wrong way.<ul><li>The Gardeners of the Tree (Verbena)</li> <li>The House of Seshet (Hermetic)</li> <li>The Sons of Mithra (Chorus)</li> </ul><i>\u200bThe Dionysian Lodges</i> are rebels, rogues and rakes, united primarily by their refusal to conform to period norms of behavior and morality. They draw influence and inspiration from the old Romantics and Libertines, as well as the modern Bohemianism, Aestheticism, and Feminism. Often referred to as <i>Rebels</i> or <i>Rakes</i>, they considered to be the most troublesome of Lodges.<ul><li>The Crucible of the Case-Hardened Soul (Hermetic)</li> <li>The Fellowship of Pan (Ecstatic)</li> <li>The Sisterhood of the Moon (Verbena)</li> </ul><i>\u200bThe Orientalist Lodges</i> are those who have turned their interests to the lands of the East, including the Middle East, India and China. As the Empires of Europe spread into the lands of Asia, it was inevitable that there would be those who returned having converted to various native beliefs and then spreading them into the drawing rooms, clubs, and salons of Europe. Sometimes referred to as <i>Exotics</i>, they are often viewed as curiosities or enigmas to more traditional Lodges.<ul><li>The Banquet of Lakshmi (Thanatoic)</li> <li>The Blue Dharma (Akashic)</li> <li>The Garden of Peace (Batini)</li> </ul>\u200b<i>The Primitive Lodges</i> are those who practice the magics of older, rural, and supposedly uncivilized cultures. While often referring to the workings of those outside of Europe or Asian cultures, it also includes the superstitions of the peasantry and villagers from across Europe and North America. Many dismissively refer to them as <i>Savages</i>, but they hold the allegedly civilized Lodges in equal contempt.<ul><li>The Bata\u2019a (Craft)</li> <li>The Red Spears (Dreamspeakers)</li> <li>The Twisters of Fate (Verbena)</li> </ul><i>The Religious Lodges</i> draw on the power of faith, specifically that of the Judeo-Christian traditions. Theirs is the power of prayer and of miracles, as well as sacred relics and the angels from on high. Sometimes called the <i>Illuminated</i>, other Lodges often hold them at arms length, questioning if they are truly Mages or not.<ul><li>The Alexandrian Society (Chorus)</li> <li>The Knights Templar (Craft)</li> <li>The Sisterhood of Gabrielle (Chorus)</li> </ul>\u200b\u200b<i>The Spiritualist Lodges</i> are deeply entwined in the spiritualist movements of the era, crossing the boundaries between life and death to convene with and draw power from the spirits of the other side. Known to some as the <i>Haunted</i>, the other Lodges view them with a small amount of fear.<ul><li>The Ghost Wheel Society (Dreamspeakers)</li> <li>The Knights of Rhadamanthus (Thanatoic)</li> <li>The Unseen Society (Hollower)</li> </ul>\u200b<i>The Scientific Lodges</i> are the most controversial among the Awakened. Having fully embraced the century\u2019s new science and eagerly incorporating it into their magic, many more traditional Mages see them as pawns of the Order of Reason and potential threats. Many of the <i>Technomancers</i> could care less, seeing the older Lodges as relics of a bygone age.<ul><li>The Children of Knowledge (Craft)</li> <li>The Electrodyne Engineers (Etherite)</li> <li>The University of Light (Batini)</li> </ul>\u200b<br /> Anyway, that was the gist of it. The idea was that future books would introduce additional Lodges. The Primitive and Religious Lodges might have even been held off until the Companion (which would've come fairly quickly, and also introduced fourth examples for each group, such as the Ruby Crucible of Thig and the Order of the Black Willow). Each Lodge type would've also gotten its own sourcebook, which would've also discussed possible additional groups, including non-Mage rivals such as the Enlightened Society of the Weeping Moon or various vampire fronts. The books about high society (the Gilded Cage and Gutter Magic) would've introduced groups like House Jerbiton and the Darklings, while the Detective's Casebook would've included the Analytical Reckoners. And there would've been books about the Middle East, India, East Asia, the Wild West, and Africa which included multiple Lodges for each region. And the Technocracy would've gotten their own book (Empire of Reason). <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-19T12:36:25-07:00","id":1508917,"title":"","content":"In my own headscanon i present the 9 traditions as less moral for a lack of a better word.<br /> That is, canonically, the biggest faction of, say, the cult of ecstacy are, if i am not mistaken, the joybringers,<br /> Ok, perfect, make them, and the rest of the progressivists, the smallest instead. Not because i dislike them, but rather because i find all of this too boring.<br /> The perfect way for the light to shine is by putting it in, well, a world of darkness<br /> <br /> Apply the same to a lot of the rest of the traditions, albeit not all of them to the same extend. ( its too bleak if literally everyone is an asshole)<br /> That is, i present them as being only somewhat better than their dark ages incarnations, in great part due to necessity.<br /> <br /> By extension, this makes it much easier for me to portray the Technocracy as more heroic, albeit for a definition of heroic that includes &quot;If the only way to prevent the return of the magocrats is to torture a few hippies, even saintly ones, then so be it&quot;<br /> <br /> Basically i make it so that the average Technocrat can point to a LOT of people in the traditions, and, even if he has a perfectly non-prejudiced knowledge of their beliefs, exclaim &quot;See, that's why i voted for room 101&quot;<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-19T17:39:19-07:00","id":1508949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1508917#post1508917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my own headscanon i present the 9 traditions as less moral for a lack of a better word.<br /> That is, canonically, the biggest faction of, say, the cult of ecstacy are, if i am not mistaken, the joybringers,<br /> Ok, perfect, make them, and the rest of the progressivists, the smallest instead. Not because i dislike them, but rather because i find all of this too boring.<br /> The perfect way for the light to shine is by putting it in, well, a world of darkness</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm slightly similar. My take one the WoD as a whole has always been that the PCs are protagonists rather than &quot;heroes&quot;. I think there's a significant Film Noir influence over the entire setting, in which the protagonists tend to be flawed, somewhat alienated from general society, and at times morally questionable. At least there always has been in my games. (This may just be the result of having watched too many things like Blade Runner, Miami Vice, Raw Deal, and Chinatown while growing up.)<br /> <br /> In the case of the Cult of Ecstasy, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1360544-homebrew-headcanons?p=1403570#post1403570\">as I mentioned in the previous thread</a>, I tend to do their internal factions slightly differently. While those sects who follow the kamamarga of the Bringers of Joy (and to a lesser extent the Seekers of the Divine) are more open and upfront about their doings than those following the ways of the Sowers of Discord or (especially) the Destroyers of Flesh, they aren't exactly more common. They just aren't as secretive or cliquish. But for every Bohemian libertine or extreme athlete adrenaline junkie, there's at least one Gnostic flagellant or Luciferian anarchist. Similarly, for every Etherite pulp hero, there's a crazy guy trying to grow miniature Godzillas in his basement. For every kindly Verbena wise woman healer, there's a Neo-Viking berserker. And for every Hermetic librarian relic hunter, there's a demonologist with a dozen beings from Hell bound to her service. <br /> <br /> In the case of the Technocracy, while I tend to portray them as fairly villainous, at least in terms of ultimate goals and the way their upper hierarchy works, when it comes to the rank and file - ie the sort of people who would be starting level PCs in a Technocracy game - I think you've got a lot of room to do stuff in that vein of &quot;well meaning people doing questionable things for what they believe is a good reason.&quot; The sort of thing that is a cornerstone of fiction like Blade Runner, The Spy Who Came in From the Cold, or PsychoPass, and offers a lot of potential for good roleplaying.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-19T18:26:35-07:00","id":1508961,"title":"","content":"The proposed Mage: Victorian Twilight book list, if anyone cares.<br /> <br /> Year One<br /> Mage: Victorian Twilight core book<br /> Victorian Arcana: Secrets of the Victorian Age (w/ Storyteller Screen)<br /> London by Twilight (signature setting)<br /> Shadows in the Fog (book of threats and antagonists)<br /> Victorian Twilight Companion (additional Lodges, additional overview of Victorian culture and technology)<br /> <br /> Year Two<br /> The Grand Tour (guide to the late 19th century world, as well as bits about the Moon, Mars, the Hollow Earth and other places)<br /> Orders of \u00c6on (Aeonic Lodges book, as well as about ritual magic societies in general, maybe also a brief look at Mummies in the period)<br /> The Gilded Cage (high society and Mages' place in it, as well as new Lodges like the Jerbiton Society)<br /> Devil's Due (Dionysian Lodges book, as well as about Victorian &quot;degeneracy&quot; in general, including Infernalism)<br /> Victorian Vampire (the Vampire sourcebook about the period)<br /> Transylvania by Twilight (crossover setting covering Romania and Hungary, as well as bits about the rest of the Balkans and Austria-Hungarian Empire)<br /> <br /> Year Three<br /> The Detective's Casebook (about the Victorian detective fiction genre and how it works in Mage, as well as new Lodges like the Quaesitors and Analytical Reckoners)<br /> Mysteries of the Orient (the Oriental Lodges book, as well as about Asian immigrants and cultural trends in Europe and elsewhere)<br /> Gutter Magic (about the poor and underclass, including new Lodges like the Darklings and the Terpsichoreans)<br /> Arabian Twilight (focused mainly the Ottoman Empire and Egypt, as well as new Lodges like the Taftini, the Invisible Blade and the Darwishim, and the status of the Web of Faith)<br /> Noble Savages (the Primitive Lodges, an overview of places where older cultures class with Victorian modernism, and possibly a brief look at were beasts)<br /> <br /> Year Four<br /> Illuminations of Faith (the Religious Lodges, faith and religion in the Victorian era, and the state of the Inquisition)<br /> World of Darkness: The Wild West (covering the Wild West setting and genre for all the games)<br /> Victorian Werewolf<br /> Shadows of the Veil (the Spiritualist Lodges, spiritualism and hauntings, and ghosts)<br /> Sunset Empires (East Asia, focused mainly on China and Japan, and new Lodges like the K'un Lu, the Wu Lung, and the Shi Ren)<br /> Science Sorcery &amp; Steam (the Scientific Lodges, a look at Victorian science, and the Prometheans)<br /> Ghost Stories (a Wraith crossover)<br /> <br /> Year Five<br /> Empire of Reason (the Technocracy sourcebook)<br /> India: Jewel of the Empire (including new Lodges)<br /> Dark Continent (Africa, again with more Lodges)<br /> The Book of Faeries (Changeling crossover)<br /> Victorian Hunter<br /> Ends of the Earth (exploration and a lot of &quot;hidden places&quot;, both real and fictional, including more about going to the Hollow Earth, undersea, the Moon, Mars, and stranger places)<br /> The Great War (end of the line and the era, about the Great War and its impact on Mage society)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 04-19-2023, 10:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-22T13:07:38-07:00","id":1509308,"title":"","content":"The revival of the thread actually made me want to share some of my own takes and interpretations on how Mage works. While they may not be bursting with flavour like for instance <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12890_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12890\" data-vbnamecard=\"12890\">No One of Consequence</a><br /> take on Victorian age Mage, they do make me happy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><b>The council of Nine is a political movement, rather than a monolithic organisation</b></i><br /> <br /> The traditions on a whole don't take the council and their ecumenical message all too seriously. The average member of a tradition is insular and turned toward their own circle of peers and doesn't really engage with the Council business at large. They see their paradigm as paramount and find it to be the ultimate hubris of the council to be pushing this weird ecumencal all practices and all paradigms are equal. Not because it's necessarily untrue, but because it surley cannot be better than the tried and true practices of their own and their teachers.<br /> <br /> In practice this means that most Mages you would encounter are deeply tied into their own practices and do not ascribe or expect purple paradigm to work. It's a &quot;new fangled thing&quot; made by the council and it distracts from the REAL business of magic at hand. This is why often troublesome members or ones that clash with practices of the Tradition are pushed towards the Council to get rid of them. Of course this does make it more likely the characters like this would end up PC's, and for most of them the discovery of the ecumenical nature of the Council to be a shock that leads them into freedom, leads them towards proving their methods superior or puts them into conflict with the openness of the Council.<br /> <br /> This also means it's harder for Mages from different pactices and backgrounds to cooperate with magick effects, and I feel that's a good thing. They can work together to fight the common enemies, but each of them is married to their own wolrdview and powered by hubris that their school of thought is the best one. To enforce this I tend to be very strict about paradigms and foci, and have in some games even stopped people from using the whole tool dropping system to great effect (at least as far as flavour goes).<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><b>Paradox is weird and not something you can expect to understand or compensate</b></i><br /> <br /> Just because the books like to default at times to things like damage or backfiring, I feel this takes away from the well, magic of Mage. Paradox should be weird and unknowable, so I prefer any backlashes or botches to actual lead to weird thing. For this reason I use flaws, weird consequences, Paradox spirits and Paradox Realms much more than the straightforward solutions. I've found it makes the games more memorable and Paradox more interesting.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i><b>The consensus is an Awakened construct and a framework for reality workers to change the world, not the actual state of the world</b></i><br /> <br /> By adhering to this, It's easier to treat the World of Darkness as our own but weirder, because otherwise a game would quickly be quite off the rails once basic causality, history we rely on and what is these days thermed as cosmological constants. I also tend to make the consensus more about reality pushing back than the idea of Sleepers pushing back.<br /> <br /> This doesn't mean the world cannot be changed, but it tends to rubber back into the general shape we as players expect it to have as long as most of the population aren't willworkers or people indocitrinated to belive what the willworkers do.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-22T15:18:12-07:00","id":1509320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1508961#post1508961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The proposed Mage: Victorian Twilight book list, if anyone cares.<br /> <br /> Year One<br /> Mage: Victorian Twilight core book<br /> Victorian Arcana: Secrets of the Victorian Age (w/ Storyteller Screen)<br /> London by Twilight (signature setting)<br /> Shadows in the Fog (book of threats and antagonists)<br /> Victorian Twilight Companion (additional Lodges, additional overview of Victorian culture and technology)<br /> <br /> Year Two<br /> The Grand Tour (guide to the late 19th century world, as well as bits about the Moon, Mars, the Hollow Earth and other places)<br /> Orders of \u00c6on (Aeonic Lodges book, as well as about ritual magic societies in general, maybe also a brief look at Mummies in the period)<br /> The Gilded Cage (high society and Mages' place in it, as well as new Lodges like the Jerbiton Society)<br /> Devil's Due (Dionysian Lodges book, as well as about Victorian &quot;degeneracy&quot; in general, including Infernalism)<br /> Victorian Vampire (the Vampire sourcebook about the period)<br /> Transylvania by Twilight (crossover setting covering Romania and Hungary, as well as bits about the rest of the Balkans and Austria-Hungarian Empire)<br /> <br /> Year Three<br /> The Detective's Casebook (about the Victorian detective fiction genre and how it works in Mage, as well as new Lodges like the Quaesitors and Analytical Reckoners)<br /> Mysteries of the Orient (the Oriental Lodges book, as well as about Asian immigrants and cultural trends in Europe and elsewhere)<br /> Gutter Magic (about the poor and underclass, including new Lodges like the Darklings and the Terpsichoreans)<br /> Arabian Twilight (focused mainly the Ottoman Empire and Egypt, as well as new Lodges like the Taftini, the Invisible Blade and the Darwishim, and the status of the Web of Faith)<br /> Noble Savages (the Primitive Lodges, an overview of places where older cultures class with Victorian modernism, and possibly a brief look at were beasts)<br /> <br /> Year Four<br /> Illuminations of Faith (the Religious Lodges, faith and religion in the Victorian era, and the state of the Inquisition)<br /> World of Darkness: The Wild West (covering the Wild West setting and genre for all the games)<br /> Victorian Werewolf<br /> Shadows of the Veil (the Spiritualist Lodges, spiritualism and hauntings, and ghosts)<br /> Sunset Empires (East Asia, focused mainly on China and Japan, and new Lodges like the K'un Lu, the Wu Lung, and the Shi Ren)<br /> Science Sorcery &amp; Steam (the Scientific Lodges, a look at Victorian science, and the Prometheans)<br /> Ghost Stories (a Wraith crossover)<br /> <br /> Year Five<br /> Empire of Reason (the Technocracy sourcebook)<br /> India: Jewel of the Empire (including new Lodges)<br /> Dark Continent (Africa, again with more Lodges)<br /> The Book of Faeries (Changeling crossover)<br /> Victorian Hunter<br /> Ends of the Earth (exploration and a lot of &quot;hidden places&quot;, both real and fictional, including more about going to the Hollow Earth, undersea, the Moon, Mars, and stranger places)<br /> The Great War (end of the line and the era, about the Great War and its impact on Mage society)</div> </div> </div> </div>Please let this come to pass! And then, a Space Opera setting with equal detail.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-24T11:53:35-07:00","id":1509506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509320#post1509320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Please let this come to pass! And then, a Space Opera setting with equal detail.</div> </div> </div> </div>Seconded, would pay for all of these books - would work perfectly with my WOD Imperium campaign","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-26T17:03:43-07:00","id":1509798,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509320#post1509320\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Please let this come to pass! And then, a Space Opera setting with equal detail.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509506#post1509506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Seconded, would pay for all of these books - would work perfectly with my WOD Imperium campaign</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you, but unfortunately the creation of Onyx Path's Victorian Age Mage renders a lot of it moot. That said, I'm sure someone who is much more familiar than I am with the 20th Anniversary edition of Mage and of Victorian Age Mage would be able to do really good Storyteller Vault supplements about things like the Victorian detective fiction genre (something that would work well for both Mage and Vampire), a Victorian edition of Hunters Hunted, 19th century Spiritualism and High Ritual mystical societies, a travel guide to the Victorian Umbra (the Moon, Mars, the Hollow Earth, the Lost World, etc.), and so forth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-26T20:49:55-07:00","id":1509833,"title":"","content":"Let\u2019 try something more mechanics-y (but system-free at the moment).<br /> <br /> Like how vampires had Virtue ratings and Wan Kuei have Soul ratings, mages have <b>Resonance</b> ratings; Devotion, Emotion, Function, Reflection.<br /> <br /> Mundane Actions (the usual Ability + Attribute dice) get bonuses scaling with a Resonance if the action\u2019s intent matches with how the mage has developed that Resonance.<br /> Eg. a bald Etherite working to develop a cream to cure baldness gets Devotion-based bonuses on his academic research rolls.<br /> <br /> <b>Resonant</b> <b>Actions</b>, rolling Ability + Resonance (ie. substitute Resonance for Attribute), are like mundane actions but with a slight dash of daily miracle. They don\u2019t risk Paradox, and even help gather Quintessence, but can be done only a limited number of times. <br /> Eg. a Hollower sees her crush about to get run over by a truck across the street, so she rolls Athletics + Emotion to jump in and rescue him, despite having 1s and 2s in Physical Attributes.<br /> <br /> <b>Shapings</b>, rolling Sphere + Attribute (ie. substitute Sphere for Ability), are clearly superhuman actions, but still somewhat restricted to the human form. They don\u2019t risk Paradox from Witnesses, but do from Vulgarity (here defined as going against the local Reality Zone\u2019s Norms)<br /> Eg. a Thanatos on a mission to assassinate a local gang leader decides to go for the shock factor, so she barges into a room full of mafioso and does the John Wick stuff with her guns. Forces + Dexterity. This is her first time using guns to kill. Her master, who prefers using actual firearms skills in assassinations (likely rolling Firearms + Function) for relative discreteness, sighs.<br /> <br /> <b>Workings</b>, rolling Sphere + Resonance, are outright magic (or hypertech, or psionics, or whatever) without excuse. Risks Paradox from Witnesses and Vulgarity, but if the only Sleeper-witnesses present are Acolytes (here defined as Sleepers whose worldviews are compatible with the mage\u2019s Focus), the Working goes off smoothly.<br /> Eg. a Hermetic chants to invoke Saturnine forces that will open the Shroud and invite the spirits of the dead, rolling Fortune (what I call Entropy) + Reflection. She\u2019s doing this to track down who killed her childhood friend, hence Reflection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-27T22:37:16-07:00","id":1509925,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509798#post1509798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A travel guide to the Victorian Umbra (the Moon, Mars, the Hollow Earth, the Lost World, etc.), and so forth.</div> </div> </div> </div>Did you have any headcanon ideas for this little gem, there\u2019s a very Space 1889 feel to the description.<br /> <br /> And did you post how the Explorators/Void Engineers are different in your campaign?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-28T02:53:00-07:00","id":1509938,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509925#post1509925\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Did you have any headcanon ideas for this little gem, there\u2019s a very Space 1889 feel to the description.<br /> <br /> And did you post how the Explorators/Void Engineers are different in your campaign?</div> </div> </div> </div>Space 1889, especially Conklin's Atlas of the Worlds (the only sourcebook from that game I still have, strangely), would be an influence. I'd have to post more later, but part of my headcanon for Mage in general is that each of the planets has multiple umbral reflections and attached pocket realms based around things like Mage metaphysics and mundane mythology/beliefs. So Mars, for example, has the real physical Mars, the Forces realm(s), the Court of the War Gods, and - importantly for this - &quot;Forgotten Mars&quot; that looks a lot like preWW2 fiction (including War of the Worlds and John Carter; the first chapter of volume 2 of Alan Moore's League of Extraordinary Gentlemen is a potential example). Modern day would probably include a second such realm based on post War sci fi (Martian Chronicles, Stranger in a Strange Land, Total Recall, etc.), with the barrier between the two getting thinner. Similar realms would exist for the Moon and Venus, and these probably all tie to the Dreaming if you want to work in some kind of Changeling cross-over, especially with Knocker \u00e6thernauts.<br /> <br /> As for the Void Engineers, I have to find my notes, but my general take is that the modern iteration are less explorers than they are paranoid obsessives convinced that the Earth and humanity are under constant threat from alien invasion (Wells's Martians, Lovecraft's Mi-go, Heinlein's puppet masters, and things like Village of the Damned, Alien, Invasion of the Body Snatchers, The Thing, The Astronaut's Wife, Virus, Event Horizon, The Mist, and Annihilation, among others). That is just the 20th century version though (and owes a lot to 90s cultural zeitgeist). While there is a thread of that paranoia in the Victorian era, it is much lesser. I think there probably was (or should have been) a War of the Worlds type incident at the turn of the century that precipitated this shift. Something they may have even kept secret from the other Conventions for fear they've been compromised. But the Victorians would still have a more colonial imperialism feel to it, with this grand mission of going out and civilizing Mars and Venus the same way the various empires think they are with Africa.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-28T05:11:16-07:00","id":1509944,"title":"","content":"I've always gone for a similar interpretation - in Umbra every Mars exists - the Shard Realm of Forces, the one from Mars Attacks, Cydonia from X Com, the ones from Borroughs and similar Planetary Romance. Which one you get to depends on what you expect to find there, but with the right procedures (and Procedures) you can get to the others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-04-28T12:06:26-07:00","id":1509976,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509938#post1509938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Space 1889, especially Conklin's Atlas of the Worlds (the only sourcebook from that game I still have, strangely), would be an influence..</div> </div> </div> </div>Conklins is arguably one of the best S1889 books, with the world river on Mercury resonating with Phillip Jose Farmers Riverworld (or is that just me)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509938#post1509938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Similar realms would exist for the Moon and Venus, and these probably all tie to the Dreaming if you want to work in some kind of Changeling cross-over, especially with Knocker \u00e6thernauts.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> Excellent idea, I'm currently writing an intergrated Tellurium so I would definately incorporate Knocker aethernauts as well as Iron Rider/Warder aether pilots.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1509938#post1509938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the Void Engineers, I have to find my notes, but my general take is that the modern iteration are less explorers than they are paranoid obsessives convinced that the Earth and humanity are under constant threat from alien invasion (Wells's Martians, Lovecraft's Mi-go, Heinlein's puppet masters, and things like Village of the Damned, Alien, Invasion of the Body Snatchers, The Thing, The Astronaut's Wife, Virus, Event Horizon, The Mist, and Annihilation, among others). I think there probably was (or should have been) a War of the Worlds type incident at the turn of the century that precipitated this shift. Something they may have even kept secret from the other Conventions for fear they've been compromised. But the Victorians would still have a more colonial imperialism feel to it, with this grand mission of going out and civilizing Mars and Venus the same way the various empires think they are with Africa.</div> </div> </div> </div>If we talking about Moores League of Extraordinary Gentlemen Vol 2 then the War of the Worlds event could have originated from the Carter Mars, particularly if the walls are blurring (as per the pre and post war blurring).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27824,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","user":"Arcanus","date":". 04-28-2023, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-02T19:53:28-07:00","id":1510546,"title":"","content":"As an aside, I have the Sphere Realms slightly altered as far as which planet they are connected to. This is mainly because Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto are all relatively recent discoveries. <br /> <br /> Sol/The Sun - Prime<br /> Mercury - Connection<br /> Venus - Life<br /> Earth - Spirit<br /> Luna/The Moon - Mind<br /> Mars - Forces<br /> The destroyed Phaeton/Astroid Belt - Fate<br /> Jupiter - Matter<br /> Saturn - Time<br /> <br /> Uranus and Neptune are often viewed by the Awakened as two halves of an Infinity Loop, possibly tied to either a theoretical tenth Sphere or Ascension itself. <br /> <br /> Pluto is, among other things, a gateway to places beyond Creation and the Tapestry, where the beings the Nephandi serve appear to originate. <br /> <br /> There was (and perhaps still is) a &quot;Lost Vulcan&quot; in the Umbra, but many believe it has become Iteration X's Autochthonia.<br /> <br /> Several of the solar system's moons also have their own unique spirit-scapes, such as Mars's moons being a gateway to something similar to the Supernal Realm of Pandemonium from Mage the Awakening, a place where visitors are forced to confront their own fears. The spirit-scapes of the moons of Jupiter and Saturn are weird and diverse, which is probably why Ethernauts spend so much time exploring them in something akin to pulp science fiction and space operas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-03T15:03:41-07:00","id":1510642,"title":"","content":"So, a theoretical alternate Mage would have initially introduced the concept of The Invisible World, aka the Umbra or Spirit World, and the Spirits which lurk throughout the World of Darkness, usually just on the other side of the Gauntlet but sometimes working their way across into the Material World. These would include Elementals, Ghosts, Demons, Conceptuals, Paradox Spirits, and other types. That would all be in the core book. <br /> The book that comes with the Storytellers Screen (Secrets of Magic) would have started introducing the wider world of the Umbra/Otherworlds and what was out there for Mages to do. (Also the Digital Web, which in 1993 would've been all the New Hotness.) <br /> <br /> The Skyvaults (aka as The Aetherial Realms) - The gateway to the Planetary Realms, as well as various astrological related places (realms reflecting the various Zodiac signs and other constellations, for example), ancient prophetic wisdom and other secrets. <br /> <br /> The Halls of the Elemental Courts - The four courts of the great Elemental Lords (Air, Fire, Earth, and Water), each with its own unique environments, customs, etiquette, and mysteries. There is an apocryphal fifth court of Aether (or perhaps the Void). <br /> <br /> The Babylon Metropolis - The spiritual ur-city, a reflection of every human city past, present, and future, jumbled together into a single chaotic maze of streets, buildings, and denizens. The cyberpunk streets of Blade Runner butt up against the fog enshrouded Victorian London, Cleopatra's Alexandria, Medieval Edo, and modern Mumbai. <br /> <br /> The Hollow Earth - A seaming pocket dimension within the Earth itself. Effectively an organic Dyson sphere surrounding an internal sun, populated by lost civilizations, strange megafauna, and unexplainable ruins. <br /> <br /> The Thousand Hells - Seemingly countless realms of doom and despair, reflecting every culture on earth, even those apparently long forgotten. Home to various devils and demons, these realms range from classic infernos and dark voids to Baroque torture chambers and post modern prisons. <br /> <br /> The Seasonal Realms - A quartet of archetypical and idealized reflections of Spring, Summer, Autumn, and Winter. Each has its own denizens (including what seem to be faerie courts), places of power, and hidden secrets. The realms sometimes shift to reflect the cultural expectations of the visitors, be it European, Native American or Asian. There are also believed to be a similar set of Realms reflecting the Wet and Dry seasons of the tropics. <br /> <br /> The Dreamlands - A vast collection of astral realms reflecting the sleeping minds of individual humanity. Some of these dreams are strong enough to become permanent realms, or even merge into larger reflections of cultural zeitgeists such as Ye Olde England or the Gernsback Continuum. <br /> <br /> The Mythworlds - A land of adventures and miracles born out of all human stories, reflecting humanity's collective hopes and fears. Many visitors report never finding it the same as when they last left it, but this may be as much because of its vast size as it is its mutability. Various gods, angels, and other beings can be found here. <br /> <br /> And all of this would've been vastly expanded upon in a book about the Otherworlds, with entire chapters on the Planetary Realms, the Astral Planes, the Heavens &amp; Hells, and the Furthest Depths.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-17T19:11:35-07:00","id":1511954,"title":"","content":"Cultural Zeitgeists within the Dreamlands<br /> <br /> The Dreamlands, where essentially Mage's Astral Realms meets Changeling's Dreaming. Born from the sleeping (and sometimes daydreaming) minds of humanity, most of these last for a few seconds to a few hours before fading like mist in the morning sun. But some have staying power, being revisited so often that they gain a sort of permanence. And some are so similar that they might merge into a larger and more stable realm. The largest and most consistent of the Dreamlands are the Zeitgeists, realms that reflect widespread cultural aspirations, historical nostalgia, and popular fictions. <br /> <br /> Zeitgeists by their very nature paint with incredibly broad strokes. They reflect a wide range of imagination concerning their topic. While some of these might include historical accuracy or other forms of realism, escapism and wish fulfillment are as much - if not more - common. When a Mage (or Changeling, astral projector, or other traveler) first enters a Zeitgeist, it can be comparable to entering a theme park attraction or film. Everything tends to be idealized and enjoyable. Possibly even a bit sanitized and safe. But the longer one spends in a Zeitgeist, the more the shine tends to wear away. Less pleasant aspects begin to crop up, eventually with many of the subject period's neuroses coming into full display. Sometimes a Zeitgeist will devolve into a total nightmare. Those who repeatedly visit certain realms report that they tend to reset themselves, reverting to their idealized state upon reentry. <br /> <br /> [If you want a really good example of what I'm getting at, see the 1998 film <i>Pleasentville</i>. Also the 1974 film <i>Westworld</i>. ]<br /> <br /> <b>The Gernsback Continuum </b><br /> A world of the Pulp and Golden Age science fiction. Flying cars and zeppelins fill the skies alongside Art Deco skyscrapers. A &quot;ray-gun gothic&quot; mash up of original Buck Rogers and Flash Gordon comics, Fritz Lang's <i>Metropolis</i>, and Doc Savage stories. The realm is frequently visited by Etherites. As one spends more time in the Continuum, they come across some of the less pleasant aspects of period politics (especially various strains of socialism, fascism, and communism), racist or sexist individuals, and corrupt business practices. Eventually, one may have to deal with inhuman Aryans or Things From Beyond the Stars. <br /> (While period sci-fi provides good inspiration, I'd also recommend things like the Gernsback alternate world from GURPS Alternate Earths 1, William Gibson's &quot;Gernsback Continuum&quot; short story which was apparently Bill Bridges's original inspiration for the realm, and perhaps the 2004 film Sky Captain and the World of Tomorrow.) <br /> <br /> <b>Hollywoodland</b><br /> The golden age of Hollywood under the old studio system. Perfect Southern California weather. Glitz. Glamour. Fame. Fortune. At least one the surface. Time seems fluid, with the late 20s and 30s flowing into the 50s and early 60s without anyone really noticing. Classic stars litter the place. Studio back lots are constantly bustling and the Brown Derby is still there. But when you dig behind the scenes, you start to find the less pleasant aspects of the business. Back room deals, abusive contracts, vice, degeneracy. Sometimes even murder, Satan worship, and worse. <br /> (See 1952's Singin' in the Rain, 2011's The Artist, 2016's Hail Caesar!, 2006's Hollywoodland, and 1999's RKO 281, as well as things like LA Confidential and Mulholland Falls.) <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-05-19T17:12:01-07:00","id":1512162,"title":"","content":"A few more.<br /> <br /> <b>Bakuhan</b> - The Tokugawa Shogunate of Japan's Edo Period. A feudal land of samurai, geisha, monks, and merchants, full of castles, shrines, and mysterious forests. Modern visitors often compare it to being in a historical drama. While generally peaceful and prosperous, with a great deal of classic art and culture, it eventually becomes apparent that the society is built upon a rigid class system, strict legal codes, and a samurai class constantly in debt. After a time, visitors seem unable to escape encounters with political backstabbing, brutal executions, and peasant revolts.<br /> See various Japanese media such as 1974's The Last Samurai (not the Tom Cruise film), 1978's The Fall of Ako Castle, Lone Wolf &amp; Cub, The Kouga Ninja Scrolls, among others, as well as Laura Joh Rowland's Sano Ichir\u014d series of historical murder mysteries.<br /> <br /> <b>Ye Olde England</b> - Medieval England, in a form likely to make medievalists cry. Visitors find themselves in something that feels akin to a Renaissance fair or a Medieval Times excursion. Knights, damsels, troubadours and the like abound. The King seems to vary, often with John Lackland or Richard the Lionhearted being common. Encounters with Robin Hood are also frequent occurrences. As time passes, some of the sheen tends to wear off, becoming a bit more realistic. Brutish knights and rebellious peasants pop up. Curiously, those who have spent the longest amount of time in the realm report encounters with vampires.<br /> See the various adaptions of Ivanhoe (especially the 1952 version) and Robin Hood. Also the original Westworld film, the 1964 film Becket, 1968's The Lion in Winter, and Ken Follett's The Pillars of the Earth.<br /> (Obviously, similar zeitgeists exist reflecting the Middle Ages of parts of France, Germany, Spain, and other parts of Europe. The one for Poland seems to have grown in size and stability lately.)<br /> <br /> <b>Libertatia</b> - The mythical pirate paradise and the Golden Age of Piracy. Multiethnic crews sail the Spanish Main in search of treasure and adventure while living under the Pirate Code. The main port of call appears to be Nassau, but others seems to exist. Many describe it as a mix of Robert Lewis Stevenson's Treasure Island and a popular theme park ride. Famous pirates from different eras appear with little rhyme or reason. The longer one stays, however, the less pleasant the surroundings become. Sea sickness and disease start to crop up. The locals become more violent. Anti pirate fleets become more common. The existence of the slave trade becomes unignorable.<br /> Obviously, the Pirates of the Caribbean films are a big influence here, as well as the various versions of Treasure Island (including its prequel series Black Sails). Also a lot of the classic Hollywood films like Captain Blood, Against All Flags, The Golden Hawk, and The Sea Hawk. Also 1995's Cutthroat Island, which is not terrible.<br /> <br /> <b>The Wild West</b> - America's frontier. Or at least a pop culture version of it. One typically finds a small but quickly growing town surrounded by small farms or ranches, perhaps a silver or gold mine, maybe a railroad camp or an Army fort. The stage coaches run regularly. Saloons seem to compete with churches for attendance. A new school has usually opened. Gunfights happen on occasion. If one travels far enough from town, you'll likely find another similar town. Or perhaps one smaller. As per usually, the less savory aspects begin to appear. Casual racism (especially toward blacks or Chinese), prostitution, snake oil salesmen, violent temperance movement activists, bandits, and conflicts with native inhabitants.<br /> The list of influences here are almost too numerous to mention. When one first arrives, one may be reminded of the films of Roy Rogers or John Wayne, among others. (Back to the Future III is also pretty good.) Later, one may find Clint Eastwood films (Pale Rider and The Outlaw Josie Wales, as well as Unforgiven) more suitable. And eventually HBO's Deadwood. (I'll also through in Blazing Saddles, just because it has always been one of my favorite films.) And 1993's Posse.<br /> <br /> Countless others exist, including Madinat Alsalam (Baghdad of the Golden Age of Islam flavored by the folk tails of 1001 Arabian Nights), Beaverton (the small town suburban world of black and white era American television), Caesarea (Ancient Rome as it transitions from Republic to Empire), Dystopia (a mashup of dystopian sci fi futures, mostly from the 1970s, but with a few more modern influences as well), Mannheim (Viking age Scandinavia tinged with Nordic mythology), Magnolia (the Antebellum South), Nevermore (a Gothic mid-19th century Eastern US coast), Cyber City (urban cyberpunk), and L'Ancient R\u00e9gime (pre-Revolutionary France). <br /> <br /> (If anyone, especially anyone from other countries, wants to suggest more, I eagerly welcome them.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-23-2023, 07:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-03T12:32:39-07:00","id":1513803,"title":"","content":"(Fleshed out the above Zeitgeists if anyone is interested.) <br /> <br /> A book that never was, <b>Witchhunters</b>: A sourcebook on the enemies of the Awakened. <br /> <br /> [This would've been part of a theme for all the WoD lines, with Nightstalkers (Vampire), Wolfsbane (Werewolf), The Quick &amp; The Dead (Wraith), Grim Fears (Changeling), Wolves at the Door (Dark Ages), Gods &amp; Monsters (Savage Rome), and Shadows in the Fog (Victorian Twilight).]<br /> <br /> Chapter One: The Natural World<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Rules for weather, including damage from tornadoes, lightening strikes, hail, high winds, tidal waves, earthquakes, avalanches, mud slides, and forest fires. <br /> Rules for exposure, including dehydration and starvation, drowning and suffocation, heatstroke, hypothermia, and frostbite, and natural poisons.<br /> Rules for sickness and disease. <br /> Animal life, including Insect/Vermin Swarms, Poisonous animals, Apex Predators (wolves and canines, big cats, crocodilians, sharks), Trained Animals (attack dogs and hunting falcons).<br /> <br /> Human Threats<br /> The Nosey and The Clueless, including bothersome neighbors (meddling kids, neighborhood gossips and snoops), the gullible (true believers, cult dupes, amateur occultists), know it alls (doctors, bureaucrats, debunkers), investigators (reporters, hackers, parapsychologists), and the Arcanum.<br /> The Mad, including insane asylums and the danger of being committed, and dangerous individuals (stalkers, violent schizophrenics, and serial killers).<br /> The Criminal Underworld, including street crime (dealers, gang bangers), specialists (forgers, smugglers), organized crime (the Mafia and other organizations, and their foot soldiers), and international terrorism.<br /> The Law, including the local police (beat cops, detectives, narcs, SWAT teams) and federal agencies (including intelligence agencies, the military, and various Twilight Agencies).<br /> The Church, including various Christian, Jewish, Muslim, Buddhist, and Hindu denominations, their priests, exorcists, and possible fanatics, as well as the Society of Leopold and other faith based hunter groups. </div> <br /> Chapter Two: The Invisible World<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The hierarchy of spirits, from the weakest fragments and minions to powerful Incarnae and Celestines, as well as powers and weaknesses.<br /> Nature Spirits, including Elementals and their courts, the Courts of Beast Kings and animal spirits, and the Aetherial Courts of the heavens.<br /> Conceptual Spirits, including those associated with Dynamism, Order, and Entropy, and various Epiphanies and Abstracts.<br /> Mythical Spirits including gods, angels, demons, ghosts and faeries. <br /> Paradox Spirits<br /> Things from Beyond Reality that the Nephandi associate with and serve.<br /> The Possessed, both the spirit-ridden and the spirit-claimed, and the powers they develop.<br /> </div> Chapter Three: Awakened Foes<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Rival and Rogue Mages, including false friends, out of control Orphans, loose canons of the Ascension War, ruthless ladder climbers, Rogue mercenaries, and mentally unstable Masters.<br /> The Marauders and their uniquely broken avatars and chaotic magic.<br /> The Forces of the Technocracy, including recruiters, assassins, robots, clones, super-soldiers, spies, and modified animals.<br /> The Nephandi and their dark magics, including corrupted Tradition members, traitors, destroyers, and even worse things.<br /> </div> Chapter Four: Supernatural Menaces<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Vampires and the secret society of the night, from lowly street punks and sirens to elder manipulators and ancient blood gods, including blood wizards.<br /> The savage and xenophobic world of werebeasts, including werewolves, fox women, jaguar men, and more, both as urban predators and rural monsters.<br /> Bygones, including ancient beings such as fey cats and giants, to modern legends like the Moth Man and UFOs, as well as dinosaurs and other time lost creatures.<br /> The Unhuman, including zombies, homunculi, robots, and Prometheans.<br /> The Unknown, urban legends among the Awakened including the Dark Carnival, the Midnight Express, and other oddities. </div> [This book would've predated the release of Wraith and Changeling, hence them not being detailed as much.]","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T11:24:13-07:00","id":1513882,"title":"","content":"And in a similar vein, an alternate version of the book Destiny's Price. <br /> <br /> The original was one of the earliest Black Dog labeled books, about the &quot;mean streets&quot; of the mid 1990s, and all those &quot;adult&quot; topics like drugs and sex and so forth. It was a mixed bag, I think. Certainly not the worst thing the Black Dog label ever put out, but certainly not the best either. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I re-appropriated the title for a book about the harsh consequences of the Ascension War and the ways it can negatively impact the lives of the Awakened, especially among the Traditions (but the Crafts and Orphans as well). <br /> <br /> <b>Destiny's Price</b>: A sourcebook about the high cost of power for Mage: The Ascension<br /> <br /> Chapter One: Physical Pain<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Guidelines and rules for the care and feeding of the human body</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Feeding and watering; effects of malnutrition, dehydration, and starvation<br /> Poisons and the damage they can do to the body<br /> The aging process and how the body gradually breaks down over time<br /> Chronic illnesses and health conditions including deadly diseases (AIDS, ebola, etc.) and birth defects<br /> Pregnancy in the game<br /> </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Drugs</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Drug abuse and addiction, its physical impacts, recovery and relapse <br /> The use of drugs and chemicals in mystical practices including vision quests<br /> Mystically enhanced drugs in the World of Darkness and Mage</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><br /> Battle Damage</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Injury resulting from fights, torture, accidents, animal attacks, and other sources<br /> Recovery from catastrophic damage (including Aggravated) and lingering injury<br /> Permanent damage and maiming, Physical disabilities </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><br /> Physical Magic</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Life magic and the ability to repair the body, including its limits<br /> Magically inflicted damage and injury including Aggravated wounds<br /> Paradox backlash and its potential impact on the body, including diseases and mutations</div> <br /> Chapter Two: Mental Trauma<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Paranoia and the cost of knowing too much</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Not being able to trust what you see and what you know when Awakened to the true nature of Reality<br /> The stress of being part of an underground resistance movement against a brutal and powerful enemy<br /> Being isolated from mundane humanity and how that impacts the psyche<br /> </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Breakdowns, tipping points, and trauma</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Psychological injury from betrayal, torture, and other events<br /> Rules for mental illness and Derangements in Mage<br /> Coping mechanisms including addictions<br /> Losing touch with reality</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><br /> The eternal struggle between wisdom and hubris when using Magic<br /> <br /> Magic and the mind</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Mind magic and its ability to reinforce and repair the human psyche and its limits<br /> Magically inflicted mental damage including possession, brainwashing, and psychic assault<br /> Paradox backlash against the mind, including hallucinations, Derangements and Quiet<br /> The special case of Marauders</div> <br /> Chapter Three: Social Loss<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Life outside of normal society</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Being part of a resistance movement and living double lives<br /> Running afoul of the Law and the legal process <br /> Interaction with the underbelly of society, including black markets, the sex trade, the Dark Web, and organized crime<br /> </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Life as an outlaw</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">Losing ones connections to friends and family<br /> Having to disappear and living off the grid<br /> Creating false identities and living a lie<br /> Always looking over your shoulder and being ready to run at any moment</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><br /> Magic and your social life</div><div style=\"margin-left:80px\">The magic of Mind, Connection, Time and Fate, using it to maintain a mundane life<br /> Arcane and the divorce from the mundane world, including loved ones<br /> Shadow Names and why Mages use them<br /> Acolytes as a bulwark against social loss<br /> Paradox backlash and its potential impact on social interaction, including banishment from reality and paradox prisons</div> <br /> Chapter Four: Storytelling<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Themes and Moods of chronicles involving stress and trauma<br /> The scale and scope of personal sacrifice<br /> Dealing with controversial topics and setting limits in your game group<br /> The subject of character damage and death<br /> Rules and systems for torture, drug use, mental illness, black markets, physical handicaps, and social loss<br /> Stock characters for use in these chronicles </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T16:37:07-07:00","id":1513977,"title":"","content":"(I'm experimenting with a new keyboard, so any typos are probably because of that.)<br /> <br /> Orphan Factions. Too small to be considered Crafts but too large or wide-spread to be mere Cabals. Most of these never achieve more than a dozen members if that. But they still have their place, often among the lowest and most personal levels of the Ascension War.<br /> <br /> Devotion House<br /> A shelter and halfway house for runaway teens in Boston, Massachusetts. Founded in 1983 by Edmond Devot, an Awakened Orphan who worked for the city's department of youth services, it was created as a safe place for young runaways and abuse victims. Over the years, Devot has found that several of his wards were also Awakened, often spontaneously on the streets or prior to running away. He has always provided mentoring and counselling, both the mundane and the mystical type, to any who seek it. Many &quot;graduates&quot; have remained devoted to the House long after adulthood, and continue to volunteer their services or offer other support. A few have even tried to establish satellite houses in neighboring cities and states with mixed success.<br /> <br /> Division 10<br /> A small and scattered group of low level cube farmers and office drones who have all been guided to Awakening by a mysterious Mister Smith. It is unclear just how old the group is, only first coming to attention in the late 90s, but some evidence points to their existence as far back as the early 70s. Members communicate - and receive instructions - through untraceable and encrypted emails, texts, and short phone calls. &quot;Mister Smith&quot; deliberately keeps his agents at a low level of Enlightenment, the better to keep them unnoticed by and unaware of the wider Awakened world. Members believe that they have been recruited into some sort of secret heroic conspiracy, one tailored to appeal to the agent's personal prejudices and vanities. Many of their covert missions seem to involve apparent nonsense errands and petty criminal acts, but their random actions sometimes result in impacts far greater than one would expect. <br /> <br /> Mason Libon<br /> &quot;Live. Leave. Survive.&quot; That is their creed. A small and extremely secretive tradition of ritual magicians scattered throughout the Western world, they keep their mystical talents a closely guarded secret. Most only know their own mentor and the rare apprentice, but sometimes two or more members who share the same mentor (or more rarely geographic location) may work together. They try to go about their mundane lives with friends and family none the wiser. If outed, they will almost certainly uproot, disappear and relocate, sometimes even abandoning their families. Those few Tradition mages who have observed one in action claim that their workings bear several striking similarities to the Arts of the Order of Hermes. <br /> <br /> The Star Council of Thal'hun<br /> Perhaps one of the largest and most organized collections of Orphans known, the Council consists of at least a dozen Awakened members and their various Acolytes and followers along the west coast of the United States. Founded in 1971, the group began as a small cabal (cult) devoted to an &quot;immortal energy being&quot; known as Khuvon. Near the end of the decade, they were joined by a group of highly devoted (fanatical) UFO investigators, developing into the current form known today. Their mystical theories are based on a mixture of &quot;primal vibrational sounds of the universe&quot;, the &quot;alien&quot; language of Luz'at that was taught to the founders by Khuvon itself, and visualization of the abstract mental concepts which lie behind the sounds and language. This is augmented by a number of technological devices and designs that the UFO researchers brought with them and continue to develop, many of which appear to be non-human or non-terrestrial in origin. The truth behind the group, including that of the technology or of Khuvon, continues to elude those who've investigated them. While San Francisco continues to be the center of the Star Council, smaller branches exist in Las Angeles, Los Vegas, and Portland, Oregon. <br /> <br /> (All of these were preexisting groups, either from various Mage books, or in the case of Division 10, from Hunter: The Vigil, just tweeked and modified to fit the sort of WoD I was aiming for, and to offer a bit of potential variety and comradeship for Orphan characters.)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T16:56:55-07:00","id":1513978,"title":"","content":"I dont know how much this is &quot;Homebrew&quot; but i have, at least in my version, a special Realm, the &quot;Ur-City&quot;, &quot;Metropolis&quot;, &quot;The city of Laberynths&quot; and other terms. The legend in the streets is one<br /> <br /> &quot;If you walk between the alleys and old, dark streets, reality and the cities starts to change. Walk more, walk without direction, and you will find it. The All-City, the City of Cities. You can wander from this unreal city in Buenos Aires and end in Copenhage. The city is dark, with impossible architecture of glass, steel and concrete. The buildings are endless, to the sky. There are things in the city that hunt people entering it, but also helps them. Understand the All City, and you will understand all the cities, my Children&quot;<br /> <br /> The idea is inspired by the Metropolis from Kult. I have more things, but are small, so i dont know if they are worth the value of sharing it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28021-camaradadoppio","name":"CamaradaDoppio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28021_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T17:48:20-07:00","id":1513980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CamaradaDoppio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1513978#post1513978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea is inspired by the Metropolis from Kult. I have more things, but are small, so i dont know if they are worth the value of sharing it</div> </div> </div> </div>I borrow a lot of stuff from other games, including Kult (and Unknown Armies). IIRC, I think the Champions RPG had/has a similar Metropolis realm which is a reflection of all cities, and I use it too. <br /> <br /> And I'm always happy to see other people's ideas, no matter how small. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-06T14:33:43-07:00","id":1514051,"title":"","content":"Sorcerer Societies.<br /> <br /> Obviously, the dividing line between a Sorcerer group and a Craft is going to be a bit vague. (For my purpose, it sort of ended up being that I had these nine groups (10 if you include the Hollow Ones) who not only generally paired up fairly well in different parts of the globe, but also ended up having each of the nine spheres as their specialty. And then a bunch of interesting other factions that seemed to work really well as different variations of path wizards.) But these are groups we generally had some fun or interesting use out of a few years ago. Most of our games tended to be set in the US, so tended to involve groups you'd be more likely to find lurking around the cities and mystic undergrounds of that part of the world.<br /> <br /> Again, several of these are groups that've appeared in Mage/WoD books over the years, and a few others are from Hunter the Vigil. <br /> <br /> Ashwood Abbey <br /> Originating among the 18th century Hellfire Clubs of Britain, the members of Ashwood Abbey pursue mystical knowledge primarily for thrills and excitement. Drawing from the ranks of the wealthy and the famous, they include the jaded and the deviant as well as thrill seekers and daredevils. Their workings frequently involve demonology, sex magic, and drugs, as well as necromancy and blood magic. Summoning and warding magic is their primary stock in trade, but curses, illusions, and even shapeshifting also fade in and out of popularity. <br /> <br /> Children of Osiris <br /> A secretive mystery cult and occult order, they date back to at least the resurgent interest in Egyptology following the Napoleonic Wars. (Their leadership claims the group has existed for much longer, but such claims are common among Victorian era occult orders.) Their ritual magic draws heavily upon ancient Egyptian forms, including true names, astrology, and alchemy, as well as necromancy. Many of them claim to have led past lives among ancient Egypt. While the Victorian sect was very strongly of the upper classes, since WW2, they've become more open, especially to those trying to heal damaged aspects of their souls. Strangely, many of them are also skilled vampire hunters. <br /> <br /> The Ascending Suns<br /> Another sect claiming decent from ancient Egypt by way of Islamic Sufism. The Suns first came to notice in the United States in between the two World Wars, being heavily involved in both African American Masonic groups and early black nationalism versions of Islam. Today, they are heavily involved in inner city urban culture, including entertainment, civil rights groups, Afrocentrism, and organized crime. Members seek spiritual purification and consciousness expansion as well as personal power through alchemy, vision quests, and holy war. <br /> <br /> Keepers of the Source<br /> A collective of New Age mystics going back at least to 1960s hippy culture, 1950s Beatniks, and earlier Bohemianism. Their primary focus is on spiritual harmony with the Earth and in the protection of sacred mystical sites, especially from exploitation by other supernatural forces. Their magic frequently blends New Age mysticism, parapsychology, and Neo-shamanism, as well as a grab bag of influences from around the world. While it is easy to dismiss them as harmless, many of them can become quite fanatical when it comes to protecting their various causes. <br /> <br /> Maidens Blood Sisterhood<br /> Hermetic feminist Wiccans organized among the Ivy League sororities during the 70s and 80s, now spread to colleges and alumni around North America. Members practice magic in the pursuit of self empowerment, self discovery, and revenge (allegedly against the patriarchy, but frequently in games of mean girl one-upmanship). Protection and cursing magic are popular pursuits, as divination and summoning. Because of their upper class backgrounds and college connections, many also hold a strong decree of social and political power within the mundane world. <br /> <br /> Brotherhood of Prometheus<br /> Those who pursue magic purely for the sake of personal power and evolution. Working together to seek out and acquire the power of the gods, often by any means necessary. Using ideas of transhumanism, psychic awakening, and spiritual fusion, they will often summon supernatural beings in order to imprison and experiment upon them. The mental stability of some members is questionable at best. <br /> <br /> The Pinball Wizards<br /> A quasi-anarchist movement of technomancer troublemakers originating in the late 1960s and exploding in size with the invention of the internet. Parapsychology, thermodynamics, chaos theory, UFO studies, and computer hackings end up as gateways into stranger realms of discovery. The political views of the group sometimes lead them into political activism and even terrorism. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-06T14:33:58-07:00","id":1514052,"title":"","content":"Well, i like the idea of Cross-Convention/Cross-Tradition groups.<br /> <br /> In my games, i like the use the concept of Egregors as Spirits. A concept powerfull enough can generate Egregors. Therefore, Economic Systems as Capitalism have Spirits, so a group of Tradition Mages, know as the Red Arrow, have the objective to find and kill the Egregor of Capitalism. The logic is &quot;Capitalism is THE TOOL of Technocracy, and by killing his Idea, we destroy it hold over reality&quot;<br /> <br /> This thing is important, because the Tradition is divided between the ones who want a Paradigm Only change, and the ones who want a Paradigm and politic change. The New Council represent the first, and the Rogue Council (Or the Red Council) the latter.<br /> <br /> In general, the Technocracy is Capitalism/Fascism aligned, more because Syndicate influece than other thing, but also because Capitalism is the Status Quo today, and fighting Capitalism is fighting their influence. <br /> <br /> This is overly politic and left-wing? Yes. I care about people hating that? No","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28021-camaradadoppio","name":"CamaradaDoppio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28021_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-10T14:28:02-07:00","id":1514511,"title":"","content":"One concept we have in our Victorian campaign is that there is a critical reason why the Order of Reason dosen\u2019t look to eradicate vampires.<br /> <br /> Aside from it having the potential of starting a supernatural war that is visible to the sleeping masses, the Order has recognised that Kindred are creatures of Static reality their presence as unnatural as it is reinforces the consensus. (And is the source of their Banality) <br /> <br /> The Order also (correctly) fears that vampires have agents within their ranks, so they operate on a mutual alliance that the vampires maintain their masquerade and keep other supernatural vermin away from the masses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-12T08:39:52-07:00","id":1514706,"title":"","content":"Homebrew mechanics<br /> <br /> Time-you require correspondence to prophesize events you're not personally involved in it's also generally recommended to have sympathetic links. Entropy is also useful to ensure you're looking at the most likely events<br /> <br /> You cannot see your own death unless its a specific as awareness of it will inevitably change it as you react to.it.<br /> <br /> Correspondence, any long range effect needs at least one dot in the relevant sphere to effect your environment. for example teleporting a human into your presence would require life 1 for a pattern lock.<br /> <br /> Mind effects are far more likely to cause quiet backlashes, especially if you're altering other peoples minds or yours on a regular basis.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Counter magic</b><br /> you can counter spells with differant spheres from the spell but you must explain how it deconstructs the spell and is effectively a spell itself.<br /> <br /> -prime 5 and entropy 5 can be used as standard counter magic by simply pulling the spell apart. As can quithlopic forces.<br /> <br /> -other supernaturals have a specific sphere their 'spells' are vulnerable. This rules tends to apply more. to mystic flavoured powers such as Rites and pacts than inherant powers.<br /> <br /> Werewolves-spirit<br /> Fae-spirit<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bDemon-prime<br /> Wraiths-entropy<br /> Mummies-prime/time<br /> Vampires-entropy.<br /> <br /> -Destiny background provides counter dice to attempts to subvert your fate.<br /> <br /> -you cannot negate truth faith Full stop.\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Nexus<br /> <br /> A nexus is effectively a highly important sleepers such as The Prophet muhammid, Socrates, Isaac Newton or queen elizibeth the 1st, a nexus effectively shapes the world in major ways as the inherent potential of humanity made manifest. Mages can kill nexus' but this will calcify whatever their objectives where for example assassinating a civil rights worker will substantially bolster the cause. Trying to use magic to negate a nexus will result in cataclysmic worldwide backlashes, for example the unions attempt to prevent the sleeper Alan turnings release of technology ahead of schedule resulted in an alternative timeline where an entire convention came into existence defected from the the union and the creation of the Internet well ahead of union timeline. Supernaturals can be nexus' but this is a rarity among rarities, more likely supernaturals will find their will subverted by the historical inertia of such beings<br /> <br /> The only real way to counter a nexus is either the sleepers themselves reject such an individual (such as Nicholai Tesla or Julian the apostate) or another Nexus (for example Adolf Hitler was countered by Winston Churchill)<br /> <br /> <br /> Mechanically nexus' have destiny 5 and are capable of having backgrounds beyond 5 and tend to have an incredible range of abilities non supernatural in nature.<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-17-2023, 07:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T08:46:46-07:00","id":1514960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1514706#post1514706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nexus<br /> <br /> A nexus is effectively a highly important sleepers such as The Prophet muhammid, Socrates, Isaac Newton or queen elizibeth the 1st, a nexus effectively shapes the world in major ways as the inherent potential of humanity made manifest. Mages can kill nexus' but this will calcify whatever their objectives where for example assassinating a civil rights worker will substantially bolster the cause. Trying to use magic to negate a nexus will result in cataclysmic worldwide backlashes, for example the unions attempt to prevent the sleeper Alan turnings release of technology ahead of schedule resulted in an alternative timeline where an entire convention came into existence defected from the the union and the creation of the Internet well ahead of union timeline. Supernaturals can be nexus' but this is a rarity among rarities, more likely supernaturals will find their will subverted by the historical inertia of such beings<br /> <br /> The only real way to counter a nexus is either the sleepers themselves reject such an individual (such as Nicholai Tesla or Julian the apostate) or another Nexus (for example Adolf Hitler was countered by Winston Churchill)<br /> <br /> <br /> Mechanically nexus' have destiny 5 and are capable of having backgrounds beyond 5 and tend to have an incredible range of abilities non supernatural in nature.<br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>We have something similar to this called Anchors but nowhere near as developed or thought through.<br /> <br /> I really like this idea essentially living reality pillars, not magical in themselves but woven into realities pattern and inertia as you\u2019ve said, and consequences if interfered with. <br /> <br /> Guessing entropy and maybe time could identify a Nexus?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T12:01:43-07:00","id":1514991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1514960#post1514960\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Guessing entropy and maybe time could identify a Nexus?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, they really spike on entropy, prime and time. Their aura is also really vibrant and the umbral realms tends to reflect their presence once they begin to shape the world around him eg-the near umbra would become riddled fascist tyrannical germanic imagery in the presence of Hitler wereas the old gods would be nearby with Julian.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T13:02:15-07:00","id":1515004,"title":"","content":"So some more of our Homebrew and Headcanon<br /> <br /> <b>Magik Resistance</b><br /> Due to the their inner power being Quintessential\u200b or laced with quintessence (vitae) most Nightfolk have an innate resistance to Magik - this takes the form of extra successes needed to affect the nightfolk.<br /> <br /> Kindred generation minus 10, Garou, Wraiths and Changelings half Gnosis, Pathos and Glamour.<br /> <br /> So this makes low generation kindred an undertaking for most Mage (which fits better).<br /> <br /> I don\u2019t weight this by Technomagik v mythic, other than maybe adding some additional effects (Technomagic might be more severe on Fey if successful).<br /> <br /> Mage can overcome this resistance by fueling the effect with Quintessance equal to the resistance level, but it\u2019s all or nothing.<br /> <br /> Effects have to have an anchor (Entropy for Vampires, Spirit Garou etc) <br /> <br /> Nightfolk Counter Magik can be performed by Thaumaturgists, Rite Masters, Occultists etc and purely increases successes.<br /> <br /> Resonance/Disonance and Paradox <br /> Our setting is in Victorian Times so we use Elegant, Uncanny and Catastrophic Magic.<br /> <br /> Elegant accumulates disonance until it exceeds their Ar\u00eate then manifests as a Resonance effect as well as making the mage noticeable to the aware.<br /> Uncanny accumulates disonance and incurs Backlash.<br /> Catastrophic provokes full blown paradox.<br /> <br /> Setting <br /> The Order of Reason fragmented in during Cromwellian times, when the British Order broke away from fractious Europe and have remained separate arms ever since.<br /> <br /> Although on reasonable terms the British Imperium has grown powerful along with the Empire, whilst the European order led from Prussia is beset by internal strife orchestrated by elder Kindred.<br /> <br /> The British Imperium is led by Lord Kelvin, or rather a Master who current form is Lord Kelvin.<br /> <br /> The Imperium however has its own challenges as the efforts of the Celestial Masters and Explorators to map mythic places from reality are repeatedly failing and worse still areas of London are experiencing minor reality fluctuations (or temporary Reality Zones).<br /> <br /> Other Nightfolk experience the fluctuations sometimes beneficially or otherwise.<br /> <br /> This is caused by the spread of Spritualism globally amplified by the Storm Eater disrupting static reality.<br /> <br /> The Voltiac League were allied to the Order but broke from the order with their denouncing Luminescent Ether, this led to a short magikal war which ended with a standoff between the Imperium and European Order, the Voltiacs using this as an opportunity to retreat to their Horizon Realm Etheria.<br /> <br /> Kelvin (The Lord Protector) has a secret group of The Templer Knight craft who serve as his secret police.<br /> <br /> Queen Victoria despite being the official figurehead of The Imperium is secretly disillusioned with them following Alfred\u2019s death (she believes they allowed him to die due to his promoting the idea of the various Orders uniting as a Technocratic Union).<br /> <br /> She has her own Mage helpers and has even tasked them with locating Albert in the Shadowlands, despite this being a deviation of Imperium dictum.<br /> <br /> One severe side effect of the fluctuations are incursions in wilder places of deep umbral entities (adding a little Mythos by Gaslight to the mix).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T13:06:36-07:00","id":1515005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ragged Robin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1514991#post1514991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, they really spike on entropy, prime and time. Their aura is also really vibrant and the umbral realms tends to reflect their presence once they begin to shape the world around him eg-the near umbra would become riddled fascist tyrannical germanic imagery in the presence of Hitler wereas the old gods would be nearby with Julian.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely stealing the Umbral aspects, if it leaves an imprint in the middle as well as the High umbra that could have some interesting consequences.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T13:45:54-07:00","id":1515010,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1515005#post1515005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Definitely stealing the Umbral aspects, if it leaves an imprint in the middle as well as the High umbra that could have some interesting consequences.</div> </div> </div> </div>pretty much, the point of nexus' interactions is supernaturals are typically shaped by them rather than the other way around. For example the ashirra and the islamification of the al i batini are a reaction to the prophet.<br /> in terms of umbra they don't reshape everything overnight but their presence causes slow but steady shifts. For example religious figures start the reshape but it typically drags out longer than their lifetimes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-20T20:20:16-07:00","id":1515714,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Here\u2019s my headcannon:<br /> <br /> The Marauders are out. They strike me as not warnings of too much hubris, but of logical outcomes to a worldview of reality being whatever you believe it to be.<br /> <br /> The Nephandi are gone, <i>as far as we know</i>. They make great boogeymen; everybody is scared of them, but nobody believes they are still around.<br /> <br /> The Disparate Alliance doesn\u2019t really exist. Instead, many crafts have seats within the Council of Traditions (more than 9). That council is not really a united front; they are closer to a nonaggression pact than anything else. Basically, they are a mystical United Nations \u2013 and about as effective.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy\u2019s not around either. They\u2019ve been replaced by The Collective. Like the Technocracy, The Collective is ruthless in its domination. Unlike the Technocracy, The Collective does not have idealism. They are not ruled by good intentions gone astray. Think of the Party from <i>1984</i> and give them magic. They are truly seeking to create a world under anarchotyranny.<br /> <br /> Someday, when I get around to running my campaign, I will make it that the players come from one tradition. I randomly picked the Cult of Ecstasy.<br /> <br /> One more thing: while reality is malleable, truth is not. At least, not yet.</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26003-richard-the-lyin-heart","name":"Richard the Lyin' Heart","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T05:48:27-07:00","id":1515738,"title":"","content":"Sounds a lot like the setup for <b>Mage: the Awakening</b>. Your Collective is very much like the Seers of the Throne, with the main difference (?) bring that the Seers don't use \u201cEnlightened Science\u201d; they use the same Magic that everyone else does, and don't even pretend otherwise.<br /> <br /> Likewise, <b>Awakening</b> has Scelesti (?) instead of Nephandi, who are less about the predatory evil that the Nephandi represent and more about the temptations of the dark arts.<br /> <br /> And the Awakened split the notion of the Marauders between two groups: the Banishers are similar to the Negation Men, in that they're capable of Awakened Magic but have rejected it, leading them to be antagonists who works the forces of Paradox against the Awakened; and a group that was originally called the Mad (I forget what their new moniker is) whose souls have been broken by their Magick and who now are a little <i>too</i> immersed in Magic. (<b>Awakening</b> doesn't have a notion of subjective reality, so a direct analog to the Marauders isn't possible; but the idea of a mage who takes the essence of Magic so far that they lose their moorings in the mundane world is still very much a thing.)<br /> <br /> Even your \u201creality is malleable, but truth is not\u201d is part of <b>Awakening</b>: they divide everything into a Supernal World of higher, mystical Truth that mages can impose on the Phenomenal World of everyday Reality.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 08-02-2023, 05:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T07:12:38-07:00","id":1515740,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1515738#post1515738\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> and a group that was originally called the Mad (I forget what their new moniker is) whose souls have been broken by their Magick and who now are a little <i>too</i> immersed in Magic. (<b>Awakening</b> doesn't have a notion of subjective reality, so a direct analog to the Marauders isn't possible; but the idea of a mage who takes the essence of Magic so far that they lose their moorings in the mundane world is still very much a thing.)<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>\u2018The Rapt\u2019. Sometimes \u2018the Enraptured\u2019.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-22T13:53:18-07:00","id":1515891,"title":"","content":"Homebrew fluff<br /> <br /> 9 mystic traditions<br /> <br /> Celestial chorus factions<br /> <br /> Monks of the studious-Orthodox church mystics, strong links with the legacy of Byzantium. have an alliance with Byzantine/Greek wraiths centered around a small settlement in the under world. Strong aversion to house Jannisary due to historical power struggles with the Empire<br /> <br /> Order of Hermes<br /> <br /> House Nocturnae- Small Necromanctic house, power has been rapidly growing due to the acquisition of relic books and questioning of dead magi following the reckoning.. Chantry is located in the Shadowlands<br /> <br /> House custos (house name is currently debated amongst the various first nation magi with the Latin term as a holder name) , a small but growing House centered around first nation people'. The house is publicly focused around the preservation of first nation cultural sites in practice the house is often focused on keeping sleepers away from sites of supernatural evil or containing them, initially kicked into the dreamspeakers at the convocation the remains of the various high ritualist cultures from first nations have been sponsored for formal house status by house Ngoma, house shea and house Hong Le with the aim of building an 'outsider' power block. specialist sphere is correspondence","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-23-2023, 02:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T15:40:04-07:00","id":1516045,"title":"","content":"<b>Virtual Adepts</b><br /> <br /> My version of the Adepts differs slightly from the mainline. For one, they were never part of the Technocracy, instead being one of the groups (alongside the Electrodyne Engineers) who rejected the premise of the Union as it was antithetical to their own basic underlying philosophy. <br /> <br /> <i>Information is Power because Information is Reality. If you can manipulate one, you can manipulate the other. Only the free and open exchange of Information will enable advancement and enlightenment. </i><br /> <br /> (In this sense, many consider the NWO to be their primary nemesis.) <br /> <br /> They have been constantly evolving alongside information technology. In the Enlightenment era they were the Sectors Guild In the early 19th century they became the Differential Engineers and after rejecting the Technocratic Union, the Analytical Reckoners. At the end of the 1950s, they became their modern incarnation of the Virtual Adepts and joined the Council of Nine. <br /> <br /> The Adepts' leader and philosophical prophet from that point until his recent assassination was Roger Thackery, a British mathematician, cryptographer, and friend of Alan Turing. With Thackery's death around before the turn of the millennium, leadership of the Tradition has fallen to former child prodigy and digital Ascension Warrior Dante. <br /> <br /> Almost all of their inter-Tradition interaction takes place online, either in the mundane deep web or in the Digital Web. Real world &quot;meat-ings&quot; tend toward a level of paranoia reminiscent of Cold War spy craft. Most groups run as democratic meritocracies, but are prone to arguments and egotism. <br /> <br /> All Virtual Adept Initiates learn the first level of Connection (Correspondence) in order to access and process the Information which makes up Reality. <br /> <br /> Their foci include any modern equipment or process which facilitates or improves the finding, processing, storage, or manipulation of information. This includes (but is not limited to) computers, surveillance systems, data networks, advanced mathematics, social engineering, bio hacks, cybernetics, quantum science, espionage tradecraft, and psychic studies. <br /> <br /> Their sanctums are usually personal labs and workstations, VR chambers, private survivalist bunkers, and (most especially) the Digital Web. <br /> Acolytes are typically those fiercely devoted to the freedom of the individual and of information, obsessive technology users, paranoid conspiracy theorists, investigators of the building blocks of reality, and digital AIs.<br /> <br /> The Tradition's primary chantry is the Crystal Palace, one of the oldest and most secure fortresses in the Digital Web. While the Palace is open to all Adepts and certain guests, many of its areas are locked behind firewalls and passwords known only to those who've proven themselves worthy and loyal. <br /> <br /> (Concepts: Wheel-chair bound web goddess, Walking encyclopedia of conspiracies looking to start his own, Underground music producer, Self-made cyborg, Bishonen biker boy street samurai.) <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T17:11:14-07:00","id":1516048,"title":"","content":"Virtual Adept factions.<br /> <br /> <b>The Network</b><br /> The old guard, upholders of the ideals of intellectual freedom and personal responsibility. They tend toward subtlety and intellectualism as they bury themselves in the modern information superstructure.<ul><li><i>The Iron Box</i> - historians and archaeologists obsessed with reconstructing the true history of the world. Many are fixated on pet conspiracies and secret histories, but are all skilled researchers, linguists, and explorers. Members frequently dig through lost ruins and attempt to raid Technocracy information centers. However, they also tend to spend a lot of time arguing among themselves.</li> <li><i>The Memory Palace</i> - linguists, psychologists, and researchers working to unlock the power of the unfettered human mind. They use mnemonics, hypnosis, advanced mathematics, and psychotropic drugs to investigate meme development, genius level intellects, psychic abilities, and past life incarnations. Many of them are deeply weird even by Mage standards.</li> <li><i>The War Room</i> - the most militant of the Old Guard, rogue intelligence operatives and analysts who carry out sabotage, assassinations, and military strikes against Technocracy targets. Operating in cell structures, members are often experts in military tactics, strategy, and logistics, as well as electronic and asymmetrical warfare.</li> <li><i>The Watchdogs</i> - masters of observation, surveillance, and security. Wired into the world's collection of cameras, microphones, and satellites, they constantly monitor activity and behavior online and off. In theory, this is to study human nature and counter Technocracy activity, but in truth more than a few are obsessive voyeurs and shut ins. Still, they are the closest thing the Tradition has to an internal police force.</li> <li><i>The Weathermen</i> - data collectors and information analysts who work to use past and present trends to forecast future events. In addition to attacks, natural disasters, and other calamities, they also predict social trends, sporting events, and random occurrences. Often human calculators able to crunch numbers in their heads faster than most mundane computers.</li> <li><i>The Chaosticians </i>- esoteric mathematicians focused on complex and chaotic systems. They try to understand the cracks and flaws in data and in Reality, working with fractals, chaos theory, and social engineering. They are often able to create large scale reactions through minute alterations. Many are high level geniuses with a distinct lack of personal skills, even by Virtual Adept standards.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Burned CIA agent out for revenge, Conspiratorial historian out to expose The Truth, Faceless voyeur waging the Ascension War through the information superstructure, Antisocial otaku with eidetic memory, Math prodigy predicting the end of the world, Parapsychologist psychonaut, Brians behind a highly successful anti-Technocracy t*rrorist cell, Shockingly conservative diplomat to the other Traditions)<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>The Information Underground</b><br /> The new generation and the modern face of the Tradition. More radicalized and rebellious than their elders, they are devoted to empowerment provided by freedom, education, and technology. They prefer direct action to light fires within modern high tech subcultures.<ul><li><i>The Invisible Hand</i> - economists and financial adventurers advocating laissez faire capitalism and free market libertarianism, with a doctrine of empowerment through economic freedom and property rights. They are zealous enemies of the Syndicate and its system of crony corporate pseudo-capitalism and government partnership fascism. Members work raw economic data and understanding of human nature into miracles of money making and information gathering.</li> <li><i>The Nexplorers </i>- one of the larger and more vocal factions, advocating for the Digital Web as the future of humanity and Reality. Master programers, cyberneticists, and creative geniuses, they bravely explore, study, and defend the Web from Technocratic and Nephandic forces, while formatting new Virtual Adept strongholds and bringing the benefits of a free and open internet to mundane reality.</li> <li><i>The Firebringers</i> - educators and activists preaching that Information is Power. Radical and often revolutionary, they work as community organizers, cultural historians, legal advocates, and professional troublemakers. Members cultivate skills as both high tech technomancers and masters of Ars Cupidae, both inspiring and coordinating social movements and uprisings.</li> <li><i>The Cypherpunks</i> - master mathematicians and puzzle masters prone to excessive curiosity and arbitrary contrariness. They use bleeding edge computer hacking, code cracking, viral information, and sheer cleverness to operate as anonymous journalists, spies, whistle blowers, and revolutionaries. Heavily influenced by the 80s cyberpunk genre, some dress and act outrageously (even violently) while others favor social invisibility.</li> <li><i>Channel Z</i> - an underground collective of outlaw artists, writers, and entertainers fighting a culture war against mainstream corporate-produced product. They frequently work subtle Mind effects into independent documentaries and films, self-published books and graphic novels, spoken word and performance art, bootleg music, viral videos, and graffiti.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Underground conservative podcaster, High tech performance artist, Outlaw hacktivist who has erased every trace of her original identity, Up and coming hot shot coder of the Digital Web, Crossword puzzle junkie turned code breaker, Angry young person fighting to take down The Man, Online graphic novelist, Cyberpunk war goddess)<br /> <br /> <b>Tomorrow's Children</b><br /> Freaks, misfits, and revolutionaries pushing the limits of technology, information, and even Reality to the bleeding edge and beyond. Harbingers of the future, they try to overcome the limits of humanity in strange and dangerous ways, often far beyond what the mundane masses are willing to currently accept.<ul><li><i>The New Flesh</i> - fanatical do-it-yourself transhumanists, they strive to evolve beyond mundane meat through the use of advanced cybernetics and bionics, bioengineering, and brain enhancements. In addition, they study artificial intelligence, nanotechnology, and xenobiology. Many rogue members of Iteration X and the Progenitors find a home here.</li> <li><i>The Reality Hackers </i>- quantum philosophers and technognostics playing with the very building blocks or reality. Investigating quantum theory, superfluids, gluons, quarks, dark matter, DNA, and neurospace, to unravel and recode the Tapestry itself. Outsiders tend to view them as either brilliant or insane.</li> <li><i>The Dream Theater</i> - investigators into the human subconscious, hacking into sleeping minds to experiment with dream constructs and subconscious programing. While many do this out of benevolent motivations, including healing psychological trauma and undoing brainwashing, others are motivated by pure curiosity or even amusement. The entire group is viewed with fear and suspicion by almost everyone.</li> <li><i>Generation Omega</i> - Born from Progenitor experiments to breed and genetically engineer psychic abilities, the original members escaped and found sanctuary with the Virtual Adepts. Members are effectively an extended family, and tend to be distrusting of outsiders, including fellow Traditionalists. Their hyper-evolved brains enable them to perform telepathy, remote viewing, psychokinesis, and even manipulate their own bodies and Reality itself.</li> <li><i>Electric Voodoo</i> - a cult centered in the Digital Web, built around the idea of temporary melding with a collection of artificial intelligences, digital spirits, and online elementals they call the Electric Loa. They work to expand their understanding of the Digital Web and of Reality while channeling the powers of modern concepts such as Credit, Nuclear Energy, and Plastic Surgery.</li> </ul>(Concepts: Second generation psychic wanting to learn more about normal people, Transhumanist demagogue rewriting his own DNA, Half-mad prophet of his AI god, Bionic bastard eager to go out in a blaze of glory, Morally questionable psychologist, Beautiful mind teetering on the brink of madness, Anthropologist studying high tech spirits, Mage who awakened during UFO abduction)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-24T17:27:09-07:00","id":1516049,"title":"","content":"You just brought up my favorite Tradition. <br /> <br /> I would add charts, tables of figures, and books to their Instruments, especially in the days before they became the Difference Engineers. Likewise, computational devices such as slide rules, sextants, and clocks. Incidentally, an appropriate chart can potentially complete calculations faster and with more precision than a digital computer can. The catch is \u201cappropriate\u201d: charts are very narrowly focused in their usage, while digital computers are general-purpose. <br /> <br /> I <i>do</i> allow VAs to access the Digital Web (kind of; see below) using Instruments other than computers, such as the aforementioned charts, tables, books, slide rules, sextants, and clocks; even \u201cadvanced mathematics\u201d can be used. The catch is that access to the Digital Web involves establishing a Sympathetic Connection to it, bringing the Correspondence Range table into play; and these non-standard ways of accessing the Digital Web have high Correspondence Ranges. <br /> <br /> And ever since <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/m/product/367375\" target=\"_blank\">Paradigm Explored: Number and Shape</a> was published, I have considered it to be required reading for VA players, with one change: the Platonic Realm isn't an Epiphany in the Astral Umbra, as it's described in that book; it's the \u201cinterior\u201d of the Hypersphere described in the Revised VA Tradbook, serving as the connective tissue between the Correspondence Point at the Hypersphere's center and the Tapestry on its surface. And the Digital Web is <i>part of </i>the Platonic Realm; specifically, it's the part that corresponds to digital computers. When a VA uses things like charts or tables to access the \u201cDigital Web\u201d, what they're <i>actually </i>accessing is whatever part of the Platonic Realm that corresponds to the information present in the Instrument being used to establish the Connection. <br /> <br /> Likewise, Mount Qaf (known to the VAs as \u201cthe Matterhorn\u201d) is that part of the Platonic Realm that corresponds to <i>human minds</i>; and it can be accessed using a Conjunctional Correspondence/Mind effect analogous to the Correspondence effect that VAs use to access the Platonic Realm. Other Traditions can potentially access other parts of the Platonic Realm, such as Verbena using Conjunctional Correspondence/Life effects to access a \u201cbiocomputing sector\u201d of the Platonic Realm, or a Wu Lung using Correspondence/Prime and feng shui to use dragon lines as a computational space. (As an aside, the notion of binary encoding is inherent in the<i> I Ching</i>'s trigrams and hexagrams.) And there may indeed be an Epiphany in the Astral Umbra that the likes of the Older of Hermes access using Conjunctional Correspondence/Spirit Magick. But the VAs were the first to see the whole of the Platonic Realm for what it is; and their access to it is much more direct, requiring no Conjunctional Spheres.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-25T00:43:39-07:00","id":1516068,"title":"","content":"In my head canon, I tend to think of the proto-Adepts as being parts of the Medieval Craftmasons and Subtle Ones, as well as some of the Byzantine Artificers, with a lot of them being monks, scholars, merchants, alchemists, and physics. The development of the printing press is probably what caused them to start to coalesce into a functional Lodge/Craft by the late Renaissance/early Enlightenment. The Sectors Guild would have been full of mapmakers, explorers, astronomers, clockmakers, and tinkerers in addition to natural philosophers, salon intellectuals, and revolutionaries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T23:42:39-07:00","id":1517034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1516068#post1516068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>The Iron Box</i> - historians and archaeologists obsessed with reconstructing the true history of the world. Many are fixated on pet conspiracies and secret histories, but are all skilled researchers, linguists, and explorers. Members frequently dig through lost ruins and attempt to raid Technocracy information centers. However, they also tend to spend a lot of time arguing among themselves.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1516068#post1516068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my head canon, I tend to think of the proto-Adepts as being parts of the Medieval Craftmasons and Subtle Ones, as well as some of the Byzantine Artificers, with a lot of them being monks, scholars, merchants, alchemists, and physics. The development of the printing press is probably what caused them to start to coalesce into a functional Lodge/Craft by the late Renaissance/early Enlightenment. The Sectors Guild would have been full of mapmakers, explorers, astronomers, clockmakers, and tinkerers in addition to natural philosophers, salon intellectuals, and revolutionaries.</div> </div> </div> </div>It shows how bounded we can become in just following the canon lore, I had largely discounted the Adepts for our historic campaign as Awakened Hackers.<br /> <br /> Having them as archivists, historians, alchemists and explorers puts a completely new spin on them and for say a Victorian campaign presents a subtle less esoteric group to the SOE.<br /> <br /> Searching for the True History of the world is something I really like, for our campaign it fits with the fluctuating reality of the 19th Century.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T13:46:58-07:00","id":1517097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1517034#post1517034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It shows how bounded we can become in just following the canon lore, I had largely discounted the Adepts for our historic campaign as Awakened Hackers.</div> </div> </div> </div>It probably doesn't help that the history section of their original Tradition book insisted on having them all use 90s-esque Hackerz style pseudonyms even back in the age of the telegraph. <br /> <br /> If the Victorian era archetype for the Sons of Ether/Electrodyne Engineers is the grandiose inventor like Nemo or the various Edisonade protagonists, I think the archetype for the Virtual Adepts/Analytical Reckoners is the genius consulting detective like Holmes. In my theoretical Mage: Victorian Twilight line, they would have been introduced as one of the new Lodges in the Detective's Casebook, because I felt that was the sort of campaign where they would most shine. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T21:39:55-07:00","id":1517195,"title":"","content":"I always rather regretted that there was never a chance for a Pulp Age line(s), similar to the Dark Ages stuff. Mage and Changeling would've really excelled there, I think, and I feel there's a lot you can do with Werewolf and Vampire as well. (Also Mummy, Project: Twilight, and others.) <br /> <br /> And if anyone ever wondered, the Technocracy counterparts to the Tradition Pulp Age characters in Guide to the Traditions would have been Operator 5, G8, The Gladiator, Little Orphan Annie, and Flash Gordon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-05T01:43:27-07:00","id":1517201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1517097#post1517097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It probably doesn't help that the history section of their original Tradition book insisted on having them all use 90s-esque Hackerz style pseudonyms even back in the age of the telegraph.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly that and had never thought beyond that, almost discounting them for our Victorian Campaign.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1517097#post1517097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Victorian era archetype for the Sons of Ether/Electrodyne Engineers is the grandiose inventor like Nemo or the various Edisonade protagonists, I think the archetype for the Virtual Adepts/Analytical Reckoners is the genius consulting detective like Holmes. In my theoretical Mage: Victorian Twilight line, they would have been introduced as one of the new Lodges in the Detective's Casebook, because I felt that was the sort of campaign where they would most shine.</div> </div> </div> </div>This works very well, as you\u2019ve eluded to less ostentatious than the SOE and more investigative.<br /> <br /> In our campaign with London so heavily affected by The Storm Eaters reality shifts, the Analytical Reckoners (again never part of the Order of Reason) would be trying to find the cause (suspecting it to be the failed experiments of The Order).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-07T14:10:25-07:00","id":1517402,"title":"","content":"Perhaps this is something that people already know about, but Anders Mage Page is a good repository of information about Mage, lots of interesting art lies on there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-07T17:24:28-07:00","id":1517431,"title":"","content":"Something I have long wanted to find is a copy of the original preview material released just ahead of 1st edition. The reason for this is that in it, each Tradition apparently not only had a specialty Sphere, but also two others they were somewhat better at, and one they were weak at. Besides the idea itself, I really want to see how this might work out in terms of arranging the spheres in a circle pattern.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-08T02:19:50-07:00","id":1517461,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1517431#post1517431\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something I have long wanted to find is a copy of the original preview material released just ahead of 1st edition. The reason for this is that in it, each Tradition apparently not only had a specialty Sphere, but also two others they were somewhat better at, and one they were weak at. Besides the idea itself, I really want to see how this might work out in terms of arranging the spheres in a circle pattern.</div> </div> </div> </div>In absence of finding it Awakenings Gross and Subtle Arcana could be used as a base, with the addition of an opposing sphere.<br /> <br /> Having a weaker sphere makes sense, as does second proficiency (SOE also being good at Forces and Spirit etc)\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-08T08:04:19-07:00","id":1517491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1517431#post1517431\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something I have long wanted to find is a copy of the original preview material released just ahead of 1st edition. The reason for this is that in it, each Tradition apparently not only had a specialty Sphere, but also two others they were somewhat better at, and one they were weak at. Besides the idea itself, I really want to see how this might work out in terms of arranging the spheres in a circle pattern.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hoping this image link works:<br /> <img src=\"https://ibb.co/y6bgQw5https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/bdn0oag10pyqszksj16q3/Cycle_of_Spheres.jpg?rlkey=gm9zhrzhjs1qj7visoyj7zbyv&amp;dl=0\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> It's the graphic from MRev p.140, showing the metaphysical cycle of the Spheres. Start at the left with Prime; Conception takes you to Mind; Focus takes you to Spirit; Form takes you to Forces, Life, and Matter; Perception takes you to Correspondence and Time; Decay takes you to Entropy; and Return takes you back to Prime.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-10T19:11:28-07:00","id":1517667,"title":"","content":"The Adepts are historically asociated with Left-Wing Anarchism. They supported the Second Spanish Republic, and most of the talks about the politics of the VA in the two tradition books point out this. Converting them from Anarco-mutualist/Anarco-Communist to wannabe libertarians fell wrong. They are a individualist bunch, yes, but they normally thinked about the collective<br /> <br /> PD: This is a small nitpick of me, but Crony Capitalism is still Capitalism. Corporations using the money and power obtained to lobby the goverment isnt some deviation of the system, is a quirk of the system working as intended","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28021-camaradadoppio","name":"CamaradaDoppio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28021_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-10T19:22:42-07:00","id":1517668,"title":"","content":"Setting the Wayback Machine to 1993, and an alternate World of Darkness. Mage: The Ascension is the new hotness this year. After the core book and the Storytellers Screen/ Secrets of Magic, there was Horizon: Stronghold of Hope (in this universe, detailing the signature city of Seattle and its surrounding area, as well as the newly founded mega-chantry of Horizon, meant to take the place of the recently fallen Doissetep as the meeting place for the Council of Nine, and as a beacon of hope to the current generation of Tradition mages) and Witchhunters. And now ...<br /> <br /> <b>Modern Magic</b>, a guide to modern mysticism<br /> <br /> Chapter One: Modern Magi<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The Modern World vs The Awakened</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Starting with a look at the personal ties younger Mages still have with the mundane world, including friends and family, dating and marriage, children, employment and/or school, and various social organizations or groups (churches, hobbies, clubs, etc.). This would touch on when and how these might qualify as Allies, Contacts, or Followers. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Secondly, the potential damage the Awakening and mystic life can do to the above. Besides the strains caused by a potentially dangerous lifestyle, there's also the issues of trying to lead a double life (including keeping your mystical and mundane lives separate from each other), what happens if you decide to leave the mundane world behind, the issues caused by things like Arcane, and what rules the various traditions have about protecting mundane bystanders from the Ascension War and other mystical dangers. Also the ins and outs of trying to bring people from your mundane life (any of the people discussed above) into the mystical world as acolytes, apprentices, or other roles. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><br /> The Modern Leviathan</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">A look at the tangled web of bureaucracy and the law within the modern Western world and the impact it can have on the Awakened. This includes state identification (social security numbers, driver's licenses, passports, etc.) and how they may work as True Names (and why it's a good idea for Mages to adapt shadow name aliases), issues of personal finance, property ownership, health care, and travel (especially internationally), and ways the law may limit or control one's ownership or purchase of certain items (including firearms and other weapons, antiques, pharmaceuticals and other drugs, animals, certain vehicles, and the like). </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Also, just what all is involved in living &quot;off the grid&quot;, just what it takes to do so, and the pros and cons of that path verses the above efforts of attempting to live a mundane life as long as possible. </div> <br /> Chapter Two: Modern Superstition<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">An in depth look at the various nooks and crannies Awakened Magic is able to operate within the modern world while (mostly) avoiding the dangers of Paradox and disbelief. (A lot of Verbena material refers to these things as mythic threads, but the concept applies just as well to all Mages.) This includes things like cultural traditions and folklore, religion, faith and spirituality, urban legends and conspiracy theories, Fortean phenomenon and paranormal events, and advanced technology and the miracles of modern science. Also the time-honored tradition of bluffing and misdirection. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">In addition, this would also talk about the subconscious effect the World of Darkness has on its mundane population. Even if people don't admit it, they are - in the back of their minds - somewhat aware of the presence of supernatural predators and uncanny powers at play. This is why various ethnic and cultural enclaves (including things like gated communities or LGBT+ neighborhoods) are wary of strangers and often fall back on many of the above beliefs. </div> <br /> Chapter Three: Modern War<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The Ascension War and the struggle for the destiny of the human race. This chapter looks at the goals and theories of the War, as well as the magic players such as the Technocracy (the dominant force), the Traditions (the resistance), the Nephandi (the rot within), the Marauders (wild cards), and the Orphans and Crafts (potential X-factors). In addition, some discussion about how the Traditions often tend to spend a lot of time arguing with each other. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">The tactics and weapons of the Ascension War, including hit and run attacks, sabotage and attrition, the importance of choosing your battles and of enjoying small victories, and the even greater importance of the individual in the War and the destiny of human kind. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">And the battlefields of the War, including the Counterculture vs Mainstream/Corporate Culture, the various cracks that exist within the modern Western way of life, the ancient power that can be found in the past, a look at the Ascension War outside the First World, the mysteries of the Invisible World and the Otherworlds, and the growing power of the Digital Web. </div> <br /> Chapter Four: Modern Tools<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Modern Foci, and the swapping out of ancient symbols for modern counterparts. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">A look at the new hotness that is Technomancy. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Various grimoires of the young and modern mages, including works by Dante, X-Cel, Bryce Grimm, Hesha Morningglade, and others.</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">A collection of modern Talismans such as X-Ray Spex, the Rough Trade Jacket, the Endless Change Purse, Lightning Blades, EMP grenades, Go-Juice, Parking Karma, the Spirit Vac, and Not So Silly Puddy.</div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Modern Bygones such as The Hook, Bigfoot, Chupacabra, Bloody Mary, the Mothman, and Sewer Alligators. </div><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Oddities of the modern world, including the Digital Web, the Midnight Express, and the 13th Floor. </div> <br /> Appendix One: Modern Gear<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">A brief collection of modern firearms, survival gear, computers and communications equipment, medical gear, a vehicles. </div> <br /> Appendix Two: The Witching Hour<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">A short chronicle involving high tech start ups, technomancy, the Technocracy, and the 13th Floor. </div> <br /> <br /> (This would be followed by Technocracy: New World Order - an antagonist sourcebook - and the Mage Players Guide, which in addition to further fleshing out the Traditions and Hollow Ones, would fully introduce the Crafts, go into more detail about magic (including specific types such as technomancy, Spirit-Talking, Ars Cupidea, Skinchanging, and others), go into more detail about attributes and abilities, and looking at creating cabals and chantries. And that would've finished out Mage's first year of releases. <br /> <br /> Year two would've been the Vampire crossover book Blood Magic, The Digital Web, Technocracy: Iteration X, the who's who book Seekers of Ascension, the Storytellers Handbook, the Technomancers' Toolbox, the Year of the Hunter book The Arcanum, and Halls of Power: Washington DC.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-11T01:50:29-07:00","id":1517688,"title":"","content":"For context, you might want to review <a href=\"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mage:_The_Ascension_books\" target=\"_blank\">the publication history of Mage</a>. In particular, 1993 only saw two books: the first edition (in August) and the Storyteller's Screen/The Angel of Mercy. The proper supplements didn't start coming out until 1994, with the Book of Chantries released in January. Ten books were released in 1994, the last one being the Book of Madness, in December. 1995 saw the final four books of the first edition era published, and ended with the second edition and Destiny's Price being published in December.<br /> <br /> Horizon: Stronghold of Hope wasn't published until July 1996. By then, the Mage line consisted of 19 supplements, including four Technocracy books and six Tradition Books. Interestingly, the Book of Crafts wouldn't be published for another two or three months. Horizon <i>was</i> the first supplement, if you discount Tradition Books, Technocracy Books, the Storyteller's Screen and the Hidden Lore book packaged with it, <i>and the entire run of first edition books</i>.<br /> <br /> Basically, your alternate scenario for Mage publication strikes me as wildly implausible, with the game appearing fully formed like Athena emerging from Zeus's skull: you have ideas in your Modern Magic book that hadn't actually gelled until years later in reality. Recast it as an alternative history of <i>second edition Mage</i> publications, in 1996, and it makes <i>much</i> more sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-27T18:22:34-07:00","id":1518978,"title":"","content":"Another book I would have enjoyed is what I refer to as Ancient Magic. This would have been a lot like Dead Magic, just more focused and useful. (Also the different name because as Werewolf freelancer Sean Riley pointed out way back when, the name is slightly problematic when talking about cultures, such as the Australian Aborigines, who have weathered a massive amount of cultural damage and are still around, very much alive.)<br /> <br /> Anyway, my ideal version of such a book would've included chapters on ancient Egypt, Ancient Greece, the Maya, the Norse, West Africa, the Hebrews, and Polynesia. Each chapter would've looked at the real world culture, its history and beliefs, mystical/religious traditions, how they fit into the World of Darkness and Mage cosmology, various Rotes, Talisman, and Bygones, and how they express themselves in the modern Traditions. <br /> <br /> A second volume would've focused on Asia (probably as part of Year of the Lotus), covering Tibet, China, Japan, India, Persia, Indonesia, Siberia/Mongolia, and the Philippines. <br /> <br /> Volume 3 would've been Southern Africa, Russia, the Celts, the Inca, Babylon, Australia, and two others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-27T20:23:25-07:00","id":1518985,"title":"","content":"(This is a repost of a post that got banned because I went in and edited a typo too quickly. It was originally posted on July 11, 2023.)<br /> <br /> For context, you might want to review <a href=\"https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Mage:_The_Ascension_books\" target=\"_blank\">the publication history of Mage</a>. In particular, 1993 only saw two books: the first edition (in August) and the Storyteller's Screen/The Angel of Mercy. The proper supplements didn't start coming out until 1994, with the Book of Chantries released in January. Ten books were released in 1994, the last one being the Book of Madness, in December. 1995 saw the final four books of the first edition era published, and ended with the second edition and Destiny's Price being published in December.<br /> <br /> Horizon: Stronghold of Hope wasn't published until July 1996. By then, the Mage line consisted of 19 supplements, including four Technocracy books and six Tradition Books. Interestingly, the Book of Crafts wouldn't be published for another two or three months. Horizon <i>was</i> the first supplement, if you discount Tradition Books, Technocracy Books, the Storyteller's Screen and the Hidden Lore book packaged with it, <i>and the entire run of first edition books</i>.<br /> <br /> Basically, your alternate scenario for Mage publication strikes me as wildly implausible, with the game appearing fully formed like Athena emerging from Zeus's skull: you have ideas in your Modern Magic book that hadn't actually gelled until years later in reality. Recast it as an alternative history of <i>second edition Mage</i> publications, in 1996, and it makes <i>much</i> more sense.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-27T20:26:16-07:00","id":1518986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1518978#post1518978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another book I would have enjoyed is what I refer to as Ancient Magic. This would have been a lot like Dead Magic, just more focused and useful. (Also the different name because as Werewolf freelancer Sean Riley pointed out way back when, the name is slightly problematic when talking about cultures, such as the Australian Aborigines, who have weathered a massive amount of cultural damage and are still around, very much alive.)<br /> <br /> Anyway, my ideal version of such a book would've included chapters on ancient Egypt, Ancient Greece, the Maya, the Norse, West Africa, the Hebrews, and Polynesia. Each chapter would've looked at the real world culture, its history and beliefs, mystical/religious traditions, how they fit into the World of Darkness and Mage cosmology, various Rotes, Talisman, and Bygones, and how they express themselves in the modern Traditions.<br /> <br /> A second volume would've focused on Asia (probably as part of Year of the Lotus), covering Tibet, China, Japan, India, Persia, Indonesia, Siberia/Mongolia, and the Philippines.<br /> <br /> Volume 3 would've been Southern Africa, Russia, the Celts, the Inca, Babylon, Australia, and two others.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would have loved to have seen this. Unfortunately, the Internet was still in its infancy back then; and the resources needed to do justice to the appropriate cultures weren't nearly as readily available as they are today.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-27T23:57:51-07:00","id":1518995,"title":"","content":"Those would be great, but some of these cultures are extremely difficult to cover sensibly in the magic department. The question then becomes how ancient you want your magic, because in cultures with oral history you have to rely on archaeological evidence and what little has survived as implications in stories or song. The Norse are a good example- there are a few sentences in the Sagas that refer to different branches of magic, but almost nothing is left of the actual practice. The best we have are books from the 17th century, which I would hardly qualify as ancient. Much is muddied by re-imaginations that have entered popular culture: anything concrete you can find on Sei\u00f0r\u200b for example is made up, because there are no primary sources.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-04T02:43:09-07:00","id":1519720,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1518978#post1518978\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another book I would have enjoyed is what I refer to as Ancient Magic. This would have been a lot like Dead Magic, just more focused and useful. (Also the different name because as Werewolf freelancer Sean Riley pointed out way back when, the name is slightly problematic when talking about cultures, such as the Australian Aborigines, who have weathered a massive amount of cultural damage and are still around, very much alive.)<br /> <br /> Anyway, my ideal version of such a book would've included chapters on ancient Egypt, Ancient Greece, the Maya, the Norse, West Africa, the Hebrews, and Polynesia. Each chapter would've looked at the real world culture, its history and beliefs, mystical/religious traditions, how they fit into the World of Darkness and Mage cosmology, various Rotes, Talisman, and Bygones, and how they express themselves in the modern Traditions.<br /> <br /> A second volume would've focused on Asia (probably as part of Year of the Lotus), covering Tibet, China, Japan, India, Persia, Indonesia, Siberia/Mongolia, and the Philippines.<br /> <br /> Volume 3 would've been Southern Africa, Russia, the Celts, the Inca, Babylon, Australia, and two others.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just thought to ask - why it is that Eastern Europe and the Baltic countries often get relatively little attention ?<br /> I certainly would like to see some books, supplements, descriptions, rules, and expansion in regard to for example Poland, Latvia, Ukraine, or Lithuania. These regions also have historical records and preservered tales and stories that date many years back.<br /> <br /> I would like to note that the countries, nations, and cultures of Eastern Europe are curious and also important as per being the people and the citizens that can deal and interact with persons and groups from Western Europe, Scandinavia, and also of course - to at least some extent - with persons and groups from Russia, Asia, or the Middle East.<br /> <br /> Eastern Europe certainly consists of nations and cultures that are interesting and worthwhile, across many various historical ages and eras.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-05T21:48:36-07:00","id":1519929,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muad'Dib</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo?p=1519720#post1519720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just thought to ask - why it is that Eastern Europe and the Baltic countries often get 